WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:00.420
Thank you.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:18.130
What did you play in the recent local elections, Mr. Rayenzi?

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.670
You got me mixed up with somebody else, Senator.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.510
I'm in a cementing contracting business.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.780
What would you say your earnings are per year?

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.340
Around 20 or 30,000.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:29.620
Say 30,000.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:40.980
You've got a $60,000 home here, a winter place in Miami, a summer place in Maine, two limousines, a sailboat worth $50,000, all on $30,000 a year.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:42.610
How do you do it, Mr. Rianzi?

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:44.890
Sometimes I wonder myself, Senator.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:54.340
It was testified here yesterday that you were paid $200,000 in cash to influence the election.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:56.330
Yeah, I read that in the papers.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.800
Now, if somebody will only tell me where all that money is...

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.180
Is the guy meddling in the election?

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.520
Does it look like it?

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:05.210
After all, you got elected, didn't you, Senator?

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.440
This man keeps getting undressed without pulling down the shades.

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.150
Well, what's your complaint, madam? Boy?

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:36.210
Here.

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:42.370
Well, from the condition of the body,

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.130
she'd been soaking in the river for several days.

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:46.030
This fur coat she wore was all she had on.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.370
Well, maybe she was a rich society matron.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:50.930
Mink isn't class-conscious, Sonny.

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:53.610
No other clothes, no identification.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.010
Well, Mr. Hutchison wants to see you in his office, sir.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:57.750
Boy!

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.580
One of those Tom Smaller's.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.420
What about the Rienzi story, George?

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.140
Boss wants to check it first.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:32.050
One column had three line banks.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:34.940
AP says the paper's being sold.

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:36.340
What? Sold.

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:37.610
Who to? When?

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:38.770
What about that prank?

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.720
That's something only the garrisonaires Hutchison and the guards would know, not us.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.720
It's a conspiracy to keep you just as you are, nice and ignorant.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:49.100
Sold. I can't believe it.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.660
Don't believe the Associated Press? My, my.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:53.720
Name, two thousand.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.300
Better run for your lives, men.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:59.020
Don't forget to trample the women, loudmouth.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:03:59.620
Record?

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:01.560
Give me the city desk.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:03.100
Look, who's the boss?

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:04.600
Who says what goes in the paper?

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.760
The managing editor, Mr. Hutchison.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:07.900
Then he's the man I want to see.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.540
Well, right now, he's busy in the bird dog.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.860
Why don't you sit down, if you don't mind?

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.000
He's still in the make-up country.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:14.800
Call him, call him.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.020
Credit controls, inflation to be halted.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:18.520
Billions.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:19.500
Yes?

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:20.720
Frank Allen, urgent.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:21.200
Okay.

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:23.410
Billions required.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:27.360
National budget, OPS, NAM, ECA.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:29.960
What's all this mean to the...

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.010
Consumption tax? Sounds like a disease.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.080
It'll be page one in every paper in the country.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.480
On the day, too. What does this tax program mean to the average man and woman?

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.220
Not billions. That's an impossible figure.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:43.240
Here, break it down. Yes, sir.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.140
What would it cost a housewife for groceries?

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:46.920
How much more for a car, radio?

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.580
Fifty bucks, a hundred? How much?

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.730
Run a story as is page one, new lead for the second edition.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:56.720
Right. United Press Pledge story in same slot?

00:04:57.710 --> 00:04:59.950
Pictures coming yet? With casualty list.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:00.980
All right. Yes, Frank.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:08.160
Hey, what about the dead nude murder story?

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:09.720
Is it murder?

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:10.700
Looks like it.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:11.620
Looks like it?

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:13.630
Who is she?

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:14.740
I know yet.

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:16.420
Got some pictures of her, though.

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:17.990
Very interesting.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.520
Put them on postcards and send it to Paris.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:24.280
Second section. Play it down. No pictures.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:24.870
Yes, sir.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:27.280
Story's fine, George. Tie it off.

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:29.970
Yes, sir.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.350
I'm late for the dome.

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:35.300
Okay. You leave tonight to handle that strike upstate?

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.200
I'd like to stay with the Rienzi story.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:38.600
You're wasting your time, baby.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:40.080
Not if we can prove he's guilty.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:41.920
It's not our job to prove he's guilty.

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:44.650
We're not detectives and we're not in the crusading business.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:45.640
Give me a week.

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:48.180
Forget it. State Senate couldn't prove anything.

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:50.040
Neither did that probe four years ago.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:51.820
You've had a nice circus, that's all.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:53.690
Television's had a field day,

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:55.570
all the papers raised their circulation,

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:58.390
and Rienzi's lawyers got richer.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:05:59.190
One week.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.030
Three days. Please, I got a good lead.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:06.690
All right. Stay out of trouble.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:10.410
Ed.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.570
That's right, Frank. Baby's on the auction block.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:17.630
But we're the best outfit in town, in the country, maybe.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:20.180
But why? Why sell?

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.980
Money. That's usually the reason something is sold, isn't it?

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:25.420
Tell them I'm on my way up.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:28.770
The heirs and the lawyers are up in the dome right now,

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:29.970
waiting to explain the nature.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.080
of their crime with facts, figures, falsehoods, and more often, they won't be drafted.

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:43.290
Mother, the paper belongs to us. Why do we have to go to court to sell it?

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:45.840
Perhaps because he never intended it to be sold.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:48.680
Oh, please, Mother. We've been over that a hundred times.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:51.120
The surrogate's court will decide that, Mrs. Garrison.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:51.480
Ed.

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:52.400
Mrs. Garrison.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:54.000
What kept you so long?

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:55.530
How's the expectant mother?

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:56.580
Lousy.

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:57.750
Hello, Ed.

00:06:57.890 --> 00:06:58.170
Alice.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:06:59.560
You're looking very well.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:06:59.980
Thank you.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:00.680
How's your husband?

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:02.060
Oh, fine. It's fine.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:03.420
It's fine, isn't it?

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:05.400
Let's get this over with.

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:07.510
I suppose you know why we're here, Mr. Hutchison.

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:10.050
Practically everybody seems to know, except the people who work here.

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:11.580
We're sorry about that.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:14.550
We thought it best to make a general announcement discreetly.

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:16.390
The death of a newspaper is never discreet.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:19.010
Here we go again.

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:22.510
The last will and testament of the late John Garrison,

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:25.010
drawn up just prior to his death, 11 years ago,

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:26.710
designated as his heirs,

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:28.330
his eldest daughter, Alice,

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:29.830
his daughter, Catherine,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.760
and his wife, Margaret.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:35.220
Inasmuch as Catherine attained her majority last week

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:36.860
and became entitled to a full vote,

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:39.150
it was decided by the three stockholders...

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:42.620
Decided? Unanimously? Of course.

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:45.190
Any objections?

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:48.660
Would it make any difference? None.

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:51.520
And I have no objections.

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:54.800
Must we go into detail?

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:56.520
I don't feel well.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:07:59.640
My entire staff feels the same way.

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:03.290
Ed, what do Alice or I know about newspapers?

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:04.410
Gives you an income.

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:07.500
We never even come down here except twice a year from me.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:08.720
You're invited every day.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.920
Mrs. Courtney's husband feels the money could be invested more wisely elsewhere.

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:16.620
John Garrison founded this paper, not Mrs. Courtney's husband.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:17.880
We're taking care of you, Ed.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:19.910
We always try, anyhow.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:21.710
You ought to get 1% of the sale price.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:24.010
Your share will amount to slightly more than $50,000.

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:24.930
Thank you.

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:28.350
You're to notify all personnel they will receive two weeks' pay.

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:28.910
Wait a minute.

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:29.970
If this sounds that we're being...

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:32.890
Who's buying the day?

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:34.070
What difference can it make?

00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:36.630
To the 1,500 people who work for you, it makes a lot of difference.

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:39.040
Well, who is buying it?

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:41.320
Or are you ashamed of it?

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:45.710
Lawrence White is the buyer.

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.350
White? We're being sold to the standard.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:50.940
I think I'm going to vomit.

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:53.820
So do I.

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:55.680
White's paper is very successful.

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:57.860
He will undoubtedly make this one more profitable, too.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:08:59.900
It won't be this paper anymore. It'll be lost.

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:02.950
As far as we're concerned, his offer is a generous one.

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:06.650
He's only buying our circulation features and goodwill.

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:09.050
He's eliminating his competition, that's all.

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:12.440
Mrs. Garrison, you've got to stop them.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:16.280
Your husband created a new kind of journalism and you helped him.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:18.800
Take a look at the first paper you ever printed.

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:22.500
Here, page one, quote.

00:09:23.650 --> 00:09:25.850
This paper will fight for progress and reform.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.920
We'll never be satisfied merely with printing the news.

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:29.980
We'll never be...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.160
afraid to attack wrong, whether by predatory wealth or predatory poverty.

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:36.620
You're not selling the day, you're killing it.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:40.570
The hearing to approve the sale will take place in surrogate's court day after tomorrow.

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:41.850
You'll be there, of course.

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:43.240
I never go to funeral.

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:48.950
I think I like that man.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:50.500
Too excitable.

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:50.940
Much.

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:55.030
It might be advisable to replace him until the sale is consummated.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:56.880
Oh, shut up.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:09:58.240
Please.

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:29.690
Lots of hollering.

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:31.420
Good decision, Henry.

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:33.650
I was sitting there minding my own business.

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:36.380
He's been asking for it. Heard a rumor. Quit without notice.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:39.940
Took a job with the record. Rumor? How about it, Mr. Hutchison?

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:44.060
Is it a rumor? We have a right to protect ourselves, haven't we?

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.740
Well, go ahead. Tell us we got nothing to worry about.

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:56.850
The day after tomorrow when Surrey gets caught.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.900
You've got two weeks' pay coming to you. The paper's closing.

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:07.120
Quit now and look for another job or wait for the probate judge's decision.

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:07.760
It's up to you.

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:13.920
There's nothing personal, Mr. Hutchison. I have my family to think of.

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:16.070
That's right, Henry. Nothing personal.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:20.940
Mr. Hutchison, the mayor... I'm busy.

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:25.630
What are you gonna do? I got an assignment.

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:29.210
Harry, there's still a sports page to get out.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:51.260
Fighting in your age. He was right to quit. They all ought to quit.

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:54.160
Maybe. Anyway, I got it out of my system.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:57.100
You were with the New York world, weren't you?

00:11:58.200 --> 00:11:59.900
Blitzer, cop, back.

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:02.080
What'd you do when it folded?

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:05.820
Let's see now. I think I got myself a drink.

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:07.560
Yep, I'm sure of it.

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:08.690
And what'd you do?

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:11.740
Came over here and went to work for old man Garrison.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:14.080
Great newspaper man.

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:16.450
Yeah, but no good as a father. Terrible.

00:12:17.930 --> 00:12:20.830
Daughters. One of them married to a high-class broker

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:22.930
who knows how to invest their money more wisely.

00:12:23.890 --> 00:12:25.950
They hate the paper, same as they hated the old man.

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.700
Get at him when he was alive, so now they're kicking him when he's dead.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:31.940
Yes? Five minutes to press time.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:33.820
Okay, come in.

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:37.630
Everybody in this racket gets kicked sooner or later, dead or alive.

00:12:38.030 --> 00:12:39.070
Get that in the fudge box.

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:39.610
Yes, sir.

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:40.910
The mayor...

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:41.730
Darn the mayor!

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:43.030
Yes, sir.

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:51.360
Who cares about us, we'll support him for re-election if we fold.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:55.000
By now, the boys will be having a nice, lively week in O'Brien's.

00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:55.660
Ever been to one?

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:59.190
Before you know it, lads, you won't be feeling a thing, not a blessed thing.

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:01.520
That's what a wake is for.

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:20.850
Hush up for a minute.

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:23.150
Friends and unemployed.

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:24.770
Let me hear you.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:26.940
We're gathered here to bear a seat.

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:29.710
Brothers and sisters.

00:13:30.220 --> 00:13:33.200
We came to praise the day, not bury it.

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:42.610
Brother Cleary, a sinner of 14 years standing,

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:46.450
sitting or lying down, will let out the misery.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:50.420
Maestro, be flat of you, please.

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:51.840
Flash, scoop, scoop.

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:57.430
I came over the River Jordan from a weekly scandal sheet

00:13:57.430 --> 00:13:59.720
and asked old John Garrison for a job.

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:02.470
Are you a journalist or a reporter, he said.

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:03.910
What's the difference, I said.

00:14:04.330 --> 00:14:06.550
A journalist makes himself the hero of the story.

00:14:06.730 --> 00:14:08.170
A reporter is only the witness.

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:13.590
Sister Bond dollar.

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:15.350
Sister Bond dollar.

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:18.000
Has the spirit moved the research department?

00:14:19.490 --> 00:14:22.190
The spirit's moved her, all right.

00:14:23.030 --> 00:14:25.270
Hallelujah, pass the collection box.

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:26.450
Sister Willebrand.

00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:28.850
Coming through the rye.

00:14:29.090 --> 00:14:29.970
Present and...

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:31.180
And half the count of the four.

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:35.850
Give us this old talk.

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:38.130
It's a lovely corpse.

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:41.580
The last poor dear, I knew it well.

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:42.780
And why not?

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:47.200
I gave it the best 14 years of my life.

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:49.010
And what have I got to show for it, huh?

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:53.710
$81 in the bank, two dead husbands,

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:59.520
and two or three kids I always wanted but never had.

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:04.030
I've covered everything from electrocutions to love nest brawls.

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:07.630
I've got fallen arches, unfixed teeth, and you want to know something?

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:14.540
I never saw Paris, but I wouldn't change those years.

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:18.310
Not for anything in this world.

00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:24.730
I see the light, brother.

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:28.480
Buy your soul, sinner.

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:29.860
Save your tears.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.320
This is what the readers want.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:31.740
No, no!

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:34.140
Throw the atheist out!

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:35.580
Don't sell it short.

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:37.180
It's got twice our circulation

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:39.320
and three times our advertising lineage.

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:46.830
Yellow, but it's not exactly a newspaper.

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:50.130
It keeps its people working.

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:50.970
Hallelujah!

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:53.650
Well, maybe if I'd given you this kind of paper,

00:15:53.710 --> 00:15:56.750
you'd still have jobs, a place for this kind of sheet.

00:15:57.430 --> 00:15:58.090
Where, Daddy?

00:15:58.960 --> 00:15:59.980
All right, so it's not your kind of...

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:04.140
What kind of paper? Who are we putting out papers for? You, you, you?

00:16:06.110 --> 00:16:08.070
Not enough anymore to give them just news.

00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:10.740
They want comics, contests, puzzles.

00:16:11.820 --> 00:16:14.800
They want to know how to bake a cake, win friends and influence the future.

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:17.950
Ergo horoscopes, tips on the horses,

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:21.420
interpretation of dreams so they can win on the numbers lotteries.

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:27.790
And if they accidentally stumble on the first page, news.

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:41.210
Oh, Paris, either.

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:43.840
Have some anesthetic, brother.

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:40.390
Mr. Hutchison, I've been trying to see you all day, sir.

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:42.690
I said I'd find you here.

00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:45.600
This will introduce me, sir.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:48.290
It's from my journalism professor at the university.

00:17:48.950 --> 00:17:52.010
Oh, so you want to be a newspaper man?

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:53.200
Yes, sir.

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:55.490
One student a semester is recommended.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:56.640
And you're it?

00:17:57.220 --> 00:17:57.540
Yes, sir.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:17:59.860
Newspaper man is the best profession.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:08.050
in the world you know what a profession is skilled job yes those repairing watches nope

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:15.350
profession is a performance for public good that's why newspaper work is a profession

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:23.490
yes sir yes i uh i suppose you want to be a columnist foreign correspondent to egypt

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:29.660
i speak arabic no sir but what you do know the customs habits religion

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:31.860
Superstitions of the people.

00:18:32.230 --> 00:18:33.890
Well, I took a course in Near Eastern religion.

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:37.370
You know the psychology of Egyptian politics and Muslim diplomacy?

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:38.760
No, sir.

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:42.750
Expert on economy, topography and geography of Egypt.

00:18:42.910 --> 00:18:45.330
I speak a little French. Maybe I could get a job in the...

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:50.080
So do I, but I couldn't hold down a job in my own Paris office.

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:51.580
I see.

00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:56.160
Joe.

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:03.240
So you want to be a reporter.

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:06.190
Here's some advice about this racket.

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:09.500
Don't ever change your mind.

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:12.120
It may not be the oldest profession, but it's the best.

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:13.890
Yes, sir.

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:39.030
Why?

00:19:41.060 --> 00:19:42.480
Why don't you go home, Ed?

00:19:43.420 --> 00:19:43.680
Yes.

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:52.180
What?

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:55.720
Gotch.

00:19:57.250 --> 00:19:59.890
I have decided to dedicate my life to you.

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:01.250
Yes, dear.

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:02.410
Yes, dear.

00:20:04.490 --> 00:20:07.190
That's why I'm going to give you the best years of my life.

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:12.980
What's the matter with you?

00:20:14.060 --> 00:20:15.100
You're a spectic.

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:15.500
Hmm?

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:16.840
A skeptic.

00:20:17.780 --> 00:20:18.460
Yes, dear.

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:21.460
White beer?

00:20:22.390 --> 00:20:22.830
Homogenized.

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:26.400
We spark it with a little scotch.

00:20:28.540 --> 00:20:29.160
Drink that.

00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:29.980
As is.

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:36.160
Nora, I love you. Let's get married again.

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:49.100
Tonight, bring on my second childhood.

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:52.280
Yes, coming here this time of night.

00:20:58.410 --> 00:20:59.090
I'm free.

00:21:00.570 --> 00:21:09.040
fired, canned. No more paper. Nothing to keep us apart anymore. The paper has been sold.

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:10.230
I know.

00:21:19.570 --> 00:21:28.750
The divorce is a very evil thing, Nora. Down from my Olympian Heights I come humbly.

00:21:31.210 --> 00:21:33.350
I'm going to make a decent woman of you again, Nora.

00:21:34.290 --> 00:21:34.990
Yes, dear.

00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:36.130
Yes, dear.

00:21:38.550 --> 00:21:39.870
We'll go on a second honeymoon.

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:41.560
We never had a first.

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:42.760
No, I'm leveling, baby.

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:44.260
No more paper.

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:46.100
Travel.

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:47.380
That's what we'll do.

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:49.000
Europe, South America, everywhere.

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:50.980
No worry about expense.

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:53.280
I am loaded.

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:54.760
So I noticed.

00:21:55.580 --> 00:21:56.220
I mean money.

00:21:56.660 --> 00:21:58.740
More money than we've ever had in our lives.

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:02.010
I was paid off for being a good boy.

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:14.370
Listen, you shouldn't have come here and work out.

00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:18.030
Yeah.

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:33.270
Not bad copy. Not bad at all.

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:37.490
What is the advertising business?

00:22:39.870 --> 00:22:41.830
You know, you were right to quit the newspaper.

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:44.560
Now you've got something you can depend on.

00:22:46.330 --> 00:22:47.190
Something legitimate.

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:50.810
Went to a wake tonight.

00:22:51.560 --> 00:22:52.880
Saw the light, sister.

00:22:53.680 --> 00:22:54.160
Hallelujah.

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:05.470
Why should I fight? For what?

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:10.150
Publishers don't care about the paper. The paper doesn't care about me.

00:23:10.310 --> 00:23:12.930
I don't care about anybody except you.

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:16.650
I met him somewhere before.

00:23:17.920 --> 00:23:19.040
Just now, in the living room.

00:23:19.940 --> 00:23:21.980
Oh, yeah. Fight? What with?

00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:24.780
I'm an employee, not a stockholder.

00:23:26.330 --> 00:23:28.150
Maybe I should have taken it to the readers.

00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:28.970
Ah!

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:34.260
What do they care? You've got to have an issue for that red-hot story.

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:36.700
Nora?

00:23:37.860 --> 00:23:38.540
Right here, darling.

00:23:39.650 --> 00:23:40.390
Right here, darling.

00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:44.130
Not to think about anybody but us.

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:46.480
Yes, dear.

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:50.600
Some great times together like we used to.

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:54.880
Remember that time in Saranac when everybody thought we weren't married,

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:56.580
so we went out and got married?

00:23:57.320 --> 00:23:58.300
The second time.

00:23:58.820 --> 00:23:59.980
Yeah, the fishing trips were...

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.720
We never went on in the hunting trips, I promised you.

00:24:03.370 --> 00:24:04.710
We'll make them all this time.

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:05.490
Yes, dear.

00:24:05.710 --> 00:24:08.290
Now I went up to Reno to try to stop the divorce.

00:24:09.620 --> 00:24:11.020
What was that you charged me with?

00:24:12.020 --> 00:24:12.420
Incompatibility.

00:24:13.360 --> 00:24:13.760
Incompatibility.

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:16.130
That was a lie.

00:24:16.820 --> 00:24:17.880
They know where to reach you?

00:24:18.410 --> 00:24:20.330
Oh, I don't have to account to anybody.

00:24:20.840 --> 00:24:21.340
Yes, dear.

00:24:24.830 --> 00:24:25.750
I don't like him.

00:24:27.060 --> 00:24:28.200
Think of a reason later.

00:24:29.170 --> 00:24:29.750
Night, dear.

00:25:03.870 --> 00:25:04.490
30 desk.

00:25:06.700 --> 00:25:06.820
What?

00:25:09.620 --> 00:25:09.880
Hold it.

00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:17.570
Okay, go ahead.

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:20.630
When did this happen?

00:25:22.330 --> 00:25:23.230
What time is it now?

00:25:24.310 --> 00:25:24.690
Six what?

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:26.300
20.

00:25:27.850 --> 00:25:28.570
When did it happen?

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:34.660
Did you call the hospital? Are they sending an ambulance?

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:37.440
Okay.

00:25:37.740 --> 00:25:41.880
Good morning, darling.

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:45.970
Ed, I thought no one knew you were here.

00:25:47.060 --> 00:25:48.740
Where else would I go when I'm in trouble?

00:25:49.430 --> 00:25:49.790
Ed!

00:25:50.740 --> 00:25:51.620
Who slept here?

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:54.180
It's time we had a talk, Ed.

00:25:54.400 --> 00:25:58.070
Not now, baby. I'm in a hurry. That call was urgent.

00:25:58.450 --> 00:25:59.010
Dinner tonight?

00:25:59.230 --> 00:25:59.970
Why not everybody?

00:26:00.400 --> 00:26:04.540
Alberta's place okay? 8 o'clock? I thought you were in a hurry.

00:26:06.050 --> 00:26:07.710
Did I have a pleasant time last night?

00:26:08.540 --> 00:26:09.240
Yes, dear.

00:26:09.560 --> 00:26:12.550
I did. What do you know?

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:26.750
I was making me around, sir. First, I didn't notice anything.

00:26:27.100 --> 00:26:29.760
And I heard a kind of low moaning coming from the road down there.

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:33.320
Well, good morning. Automobile accident, eh?

00:26:33.500 --> 00:26:34.380
Get him to the hospital.

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:35.200
Name?

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:36.720
I said get him to the hospital.

00:26:37.180 --> 00:26:37.640
Wait a minute.

00:26:38.610 --> 00:26:42.670
My name is Burroughs. I work for the day. He runs it.

00:26:43.790 --> 00:26:44.750
Oh, Ben.

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:53.240
Started banging me around when they got me in the car.

00:26:54.580 --> 00:26:57.880
How many were there? Three? Maybe four?

00:26:57.960 --> 00:26:59.240
Well, what was it? Three or four?

00:26:59.260 --> 00:26:59.800
I don't know.

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:03.160
They gave you a going over. What with?

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:04.280
Fists.

00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:07.030
One of them hit me in the face with something hard.

00:27:07.330 --> 00:27:08.230
A sap, I guess.

00:27:08.470 --> 00:27:09.130
What did they say?

00:27:09.540 --> 00:27:11.380
Nothing. Not after they got me in the car.

00:27:11.540 --> 00:27:11.860
Before?

00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:14.890
Yes. Outside the Hall of Records. I told you.

00:27:14.970 --> 00:27:15.610
Tell me again.

00:27:15.770 --> 00:27:16.350
Please, sir. He's helping.

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:18.220
Shut up. The Hall of Records.

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:22.720
You went there to check on Rienzi. George.

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:26.460
The man I talked to must have tipped Rienzi.

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:27.880
Yeah? How do you know?

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:29.860
Left the office for a few minutes.

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:31.030
Probably phoned.

00:27:31.230 --> 00:27:32.510
Probably, but you're not positive.

00:27:32.710 --> 00:27:32.830
No.

00:27:33.580 --> 00:27:34.480
Stand up in court.

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:36.800
How else were they waiting for me when I came out?

00:27:37.930 --> 00:27:38.570
Who was waiting?

00:27:39.110 --> 00:27:39.690
Rienzi's men.

00:27:40.580 --> 00:27:41.100
Identify them?

00:27:41.800 --> 00:27:42.380
One, maybe.

00:27:43.510 --> 00:27:45.010
Former boxer, torpedo.

00:27:46.180 --> 00:27:46.800
Know his name?

00:27:48.210 --> 00:27:48.570
Whitey.

00:27:48.850 --> 00:27:50.710
I'm not sure Whitey's something.

00:27:50.810 --> 00:27:52.390
If I brought him in, could you identify him?

00:27:53.050 --> 00:27:54.370
He asked me if I was Burroughs.

00:27:54.410 --> 00:27:55.710
He asked me if I worked for the day.

00:27:56.480 --> 00:27:57.400
What my car was it?

00:27:57.540 --> 00:27:58.020
I don't know.

00:27:58.140 --> 00:27:59.320
Sedan, blue, black, white.

00:27:59.560 --> 00:27:59.980
What are you trying to do?

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:01.260
What are you trying to do? Protect Rienzi?

00:28:01.340 --> 00:28:02.780
I want facts that won't bounce.

00:28:03.590 --> 00:28:06.370
Facts that'll stand up against Rienzi's lawyers and libel suits.

00:28:06.510 --> 00:28:08.510
Facts that'll tear Rienzi's syndicate wide open.

00:28:09.460 --> 00:28:11.040
It just can't be any mistake.

00:28:11.240 --> 00:28:12.540
We can't have any retractions.

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:34.510
How's it look? Afraid he may lose that eye.

00:28:37.420 --> 00:28:37.900
Burroughs?

00:28:39.570 --> 00:28:41.950
Go away, Mr. Hutchison. Let us alone.

00:28:42.270 --> 00:28:43.110
Are you blaming me?

00:28:44.060 --> 00:28:45.120
Who sent him out?

00:28:45.590 --> 00:28:46.410
And for what?

00:28:47.190 --> 00:28:49.250
Great big fat glory of a newspaper?

00:28:49.980 --> 00:28:51.720
Paper you haven't even got anymore.

00:28:56.040 --> 00:28:57.180
Mr. Allen, what's the day?

00:28:57.400 --> 00:28:57.860
Good morning.

00:28:58.300 --> 00:28:58.760
One moment.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:01.820
Every man you can spare on the Rienzi story.

00:29:01.980 --> 00:29:06.460
Picture layout, the work, where he gets his money, his tie-up, data, facts, facts and more facts.

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:08.120
The tough thing is to prove them then.

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:08.840
Prove them later.

00:29:10.880 --> 00:29:11.220
Charlie.

00:29:12.070 --> 00:29:12.350
Boy.

00:29:13.080 --> 00:29:13.880
City Morb.

00:29:14.260 --> 00:29:15.800
This is Willebrand of the day.

00:29:16.260 --> 00:29:19.200
Any identification yet on that nude in the fur coat?

00:29:20.570 --> 00:29:21.130
Born dollar.

00:29:21.490 --> 00:29:21.770
Relax.

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:24.670
Ever heard of Rienzi?

00:29:25.330 --> 00:29:25.850
Okay, now.

00:29:26.230 --> 00:29:29.850
Rienzi Tomas, 51, born Palermo, Sicily.

00:29:30.350 --> 00:29:33.930
Emigrated here in 1914, attended public school number 47,

00:29:34.510 --> 00:29:36.510
has two children by legal wife Gertrude.

00:29:36.550 --> 00:29:38.270
We're not proposing him for the Chamber of Commerce.

00:29:38.410 --> 00:29:40.430
We want to convict him of every known crime in the books,

00:29:40.430 --> 00:29:43.410
all of which he's committed, and some even you, even I, never heard of.

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:44.170
I want everything.

00:29:44.540 --> 00:29:44.980
Yes, sir.

00:29:45.320 --> 00:29:48.280
I must have taken 20 shots of her as they were dragging her out of the river.

00:29:48.480 --> 00:29:50.600
That fur coat's worth five or six thousand dollars.

00:29:50.700 --> 00:29:52.800
Get your camera, cover for us in the city hospital.

00:29:53.100 --> 00:29:54.450
George? Our George?

00:29:54.630 --> 00:29:57.750
That's right. You take Rienzi, his wife, home, cars, everything.

00:29:57.990 --> 00:29:59.950
What if he smashes the camera? He's done it before.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.580
Let him. The picture's of him doing it.

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:09.160
Bill, I want a cartoon on Rianzi.

00:30:09.480 --> 00:30:11.420
He's got to be hard, tough, below the belt.

00:30:11.900 --> 00:30:14.720
Vulture sucking the life out of a city. You got it?

00:30:15.760 --> 00:30:17.820
But a vulture only preys on the dead or the dying.

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:25.280
Preys. Here's your caption. Let us pray. D-R-E-Y.

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:27.080
Hey, but why should I stick my neck out?

00:30:27.370 --> 00:30:28.450
I want it for the first edition.

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:33.370
I get in a jam with Rienzi, and tomorrow the paper folds anyway.

00:30:33.570 --> 00:30:34.510
Where does that leave me?

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:35.960
You're fired.

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:37.920
Wait a minute and slam off and get him out of here.

00:30:38.020 --> 00:30:39.840
Why, the excitement. Everybody knows we're washed up.

00:30:39.920 --> 00:30:40.600
That's your mistake.

00:30:40.700 --> 00:30:41.880
But I worked here four years.

00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:42.880
That's my mistake.

00:30:45.100 --> 00:30:47.660
Say that. What you about there?

00:30:49.620 --> 00:30:51.260
Trantley, get your stenotype in there.

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:53.160
Get Dr. Emanuel on the phone.

00:30:53.960 --> 00:30:57.160
Throw $500 out of my bank and get it over to Burroughs' wife.

00:30:57.960 --> 00:30:59.420
Mr. Bellamy here's been waiting.

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:03.800
Rewrite desk. Lobster ship.

00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:05.800
What's the lobster ship?

00:31:06.120 --> 00:31:09.290
After midnight, we circled out with the thermidor and that.

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:15.820
Page one editorial, ten-point type, double column.

00:31:17.560 --> 00:31:25.990
Byline, set off in boldface will be John Garrison.

00:31:27.300 --> 00:31:27.420
Quote.

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:35.210
I am dead. I've been dead for 11 years.

00:31:36.690 --> 00:31:39.130
By tomorrow, this newspaper may also be dead.

00:31:40.630 --> 00:31:44.580
But as long as it lives, the day, continue to report the facts

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:50.000
and the meaning of those facts without fear, without distortion,

00:31:50.630 --> 00:31:54.120
without hope of personal gain, as it always has done.

00:31:56.940 --> 00:31:57.280
Yes?

00:31:57.600 --> 00:31:58.940
Dr. Emanuel on two.

00:32:01.660 --> 00:32:02.360
Oh, hello, doctor.

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:05.020
No, no, no, no, I'm fine.

00:32:06.280 --> 00:32:08.150
Yeah, uh, I want to ask you a favor.

00:32:09.260 --> 00:32:11.020
Personal friend of mine needs your help.

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:13.120
He's in the city hospital.

00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:16.200
Name is Burroughs, George Burroughs.

00:32:17.390 --> 00:32:17.590
What?

00:32:19.880 --> 00:32:21.720
What is more important, doctor?

00:32:21.860 --> 00:32:24.840
You're delivering a lecture in London or saving a man's eyesight.

00:32:25.910 --> 00:32:26.730
Well, cancel it.

00:32:27.810 --> 00:32:28.550
Well, then delay it.

00:32:30.510 --> 00:32:32.490
We weren't too busy to raise funds for your clinic.

00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:34.980
Well, certainly I'm putting it on a personal basis.

00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:36.640
What's a friend for, if not for a favor?

00:32:38.330 --> 00:32:42.460
Thank you. Real humanitarian.

00:32:47.060 --> 00:32:47.580
Where was I?

00:32:48.890 --> 00:32:51.870
We'll continue to report the facts and the meaning of those facts.

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:54.520
Oh, that sentence is too long. Break it down.

00:32:55.510 --> 00:32:57.810
Change the word distortion. Somebody mightn't know what it means.

00:32:58.820 --> 00:32:59.100
Okay?

00:33:00.220 --> 00:33:01.300
Paragraph, quote,

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:04.540
What are the facts?

00:33:06.250 --> 00:33:07.970
Lienzi stuffs your ballot boxes.

00:33:40.520 --> 00:33:41.820
Save the paper, this is it.

00:33:51.930 --> 00:33:52.430
Paper yet?

00:33:54.870 --> 00:33:55.510
Who's responsible?

00:33:58.080 --> 00:33:58.440
Hutchison.

00:34:00.320 --> 00:34:04.880
Well, talk to him. Why can't you?

00:34:08.100 --> 00:34:12.960
I want to meet him personally. Why not?

00:34:17.240 --> 00:34:20.040
Everybody can be reached. Remember, Judge?

00:34:24.620 --> 00:34:27.240
Give me a three-column lead, a two-line bank on that ray.

00:34:27.540 --> 00:34:29.970
Evening, Mr. White. Did you see this spread on Rienzi?

00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:31.000
Yes, sir.

00:34:31.320 --> 00:34:32.720
What have you done about it for our paper?

00:34:32.880 --> 00:34:33.260
Done, sir.

00:34:33.400 --> 00:34:34.920
A reporter gets into a barroom brawl.

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:36.460
They say it was Rienzi.

00:34:36.560 --> 00:34:37.320
But can they prove it?

00:34:37.780 --> 00:34:38.200
Prove it?

00:34:38.320 --> 00:34:39.460
Front page editorials.

00:34:39.640 --> 00:34:40.280
Splashy cartoons.

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:41.820
Why, it's old fashioned, Mr. White.

00:34:42.330 --> 00:34:45.070
And what's so ultra modern about this horse in our paper?

00:34:46.190 --> 00:34:48.770
Then give me some old fashioned journalism in the standard.

00:34:49.100 --> 00:34:49.600
Yes, sir.

00:34:52.360 --> 00:34:53.220
Give me the city hospital.

00:34:56.940 --> 00:34:57.720
Sorry, I'm late.

00:34:58.200 --> 00:34:59.050
You like it?

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:01.420
The best-looking front page in town.

00:35:01.640 --> 00:35:02.440
As usual.

00:35:03.020 --> 00:35:07.560
The make-up, the cartoon, the editorial under the name of Garrison.

00:35:07.840 --> 00:35:09.120
Yours? That's wonderful.

00:35:09.600 --> 00:35:13.090
As usual. How are we? Are we as usual?

00:35:13.950 --> 00:35:15.670
Maybe the heirs will sit up and take notice.

00:35:15.910 --> 00:35:16.310
Of us?

00:35:16.950 --> 00:35:20.250
They won't sell the paper, not now, not in the middle of a fight like this.

00:35:20.370 --> 00:35:21.850
It'd be like endorsing Rienzig.

00:35:22.030 --> 00:35:23.370
A wonderful dress for dinner.

00:35:25.020 --> 00:35:26.560
You look much better than you did last night.

00:35:26.940 --> 00:35:27.600
How do you feel?

00:35:28.120 --> 00:35:28.560
Amorous.

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:29.800
Good evening, Mrs. Hutchison.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:32.900
Mr. Hutchison, your table's ready, sir. Will you order now?

00:35:33.230 --> 00:35:34.350
An appetizer first, perhaps?

00:35:34.630 --> 00:35:36.830
Oh, no, thank you. I have mine.

00:35:37.830 --> 00:35:39.090
I'll just steak for me.

00:35:39.250 --> 00:35:40.190
Telephone, Mr. Hutchison.

00:35:40.490 --> 00:35:41.410
Tell him I'm feeding.

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:43.500
They said it's important.

00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:46.100
Urgent, dear, as usual.

00:35:52.460 --> 00:35:52.860
Hutchison.

00:35:53.040 --> 00:35:53.340
Yes, sir.

00:35:54.600 --> 00:35:55.300
Mrs. Hutchison.

00:36:00.250 --> 00:36:00.590
Yes?

00:36:01.310 --> 00:36:03.070
This is Willebrand. I'm at the city morgue.

00:36:03.270 --> 00:36:05.350
The place where little girls check their fur coats.

00:36:07.310 --> 00:36:08.590
The dead nude? What about her?

00:36:09.370 --> 00:36:13.640
Well, her mother showed up to identify her this afternoon as a Mrs. Schmidt.

00:36:14.480 --> 00:36:16.020
That's the girl's name, Bessie Schmidt.

00:36:16.490 --> 00:36:19.350
But she also used the name Sally Gardner.

00:36:20.060 --> 00:36:21.870
Why bother me? Write it.

00:36:22.100 --> 00:36:23.980
Well, what I wanted to know was this.

00:36:24.320 --> 00:36:27.950
Now, Alan said he thought I ought to check with you first, but, um...

00:36:30.090 --> 00:36:33.230
This Mrs. Schmidt knows a lot more than she's telling.

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:39.830
No, no, but I thought maybe if you talked to her, you could get...

00:36:39.830 --> 00:36:41.690
Let's not be dramatic, Mrs. Willebrand.

00:36:41.980 --> 00:36:43.380
No, no, no, I can't. You handle it.

00:36:43.580 --> 00:36:59.700
Her name's Fifi. She made a pass at me in the cloakroom.

00:37:00.280 --> 00:37:14.260
way i have with women i'm getting married again ed right i'm giving it to you straight and fast

00:37:14.260 --> 00:37:22.000
you don't know the man he's my boss at the advertising agency first me now another boss

00:37:22.000 --> 00:37:26.840
it's getting to be a habit with you isn't it like you to meet him compare notes you mean

00:37:27.180 --> 00:37:29.910
thanks i know enough people already his name

00:37:30.520 --> 00:37:32.040
I don't want to know anything about him.

00:37:32.280 --> 00:37:33.600
I told him all about you.

00:37:33.740 --> 00:37:34.120
Everything.

00:37:34.440 --> 00:37:35.940
Sit down.

00:37:38.210 --> 00:37:39.890
I'm not one of your modern husbands.

00:37:40.050 --> 00:37:42.070
Chin-up, stiff-up lip and all that sort of stuff.

00:37:42.700 --> 00:37:44.520
Always ready to discuss things sensibly.

00:37:44.700 --> 00:37:47.300
There isn't anything to discuss. I don't need your consent.

00:37:47.860 --> 00:37:49.460
We're divorced. Have been for two years.

00:37:49.460 --> 00:37:50.420
I don't recognize a divorce.

00:37:50.620 --> 00:37:51.580
You agreed to it.

00:37:51.660 --> 00:37:52.320
I was wrong.

00:37:53.100 --> 00:37:53.780
You're my wife.

00:37:54.740 --> 00:37:56.980
Not only because somebody said a few words over us,

00:37:57.060 --> 00:37:59.540
but because of all we meant to each other for eight years.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:03.900
change all that with more words legal or otherwise they want you on the

00:38:03.900 --> 00:38:08.520
telephone mr. Hutchison no way you want me to quit the paper okay I'll get

00:38:08.520 --> 00:38:13.520
another job something I have to go back today tomorrow it's over it's right that

00:38:13.520 --> 00:38:17.600
you should go back it's where you belong you're the best newspaper man in the

00:38:17.600 --> 00:38:24.580
world don't want to change that I never did your whole life and for you it's

00:38:24.580 --> 00:38:28.930
right gotta write it to a life tune you can't give it

00:38:30.960 --> 00:38:31.340
Can he?

00:38:32.210 --> 00:38:32.610
Maybe.

00:38:34.640 --> 00:38:36.680
Can you be the same with him as you were with me?

00:38:37.720 --> 00:38:45.630
Is it that easy to love him or you don't?

00:38:46.140 --> 00:38:47.020
Not the same way.

00:38:48.670 --> 00:38:50.970
Maybe love isn't enough to make a marriage worse.

00:38:53.280 --> 00:38:58.480
Mr. Hutchison, I'm sorry to bother you again, but it's your office, Mr. Allen.

00:38:59.300 --> 00:38:59.960
He said if you want...

00:39:00.060 --> 00:39:01.580
come to the phone than to come back to the paper.

00:39:03.690 --> 00:39:04.190
I'm sorry, sir.

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:23.930
Yes, what?

00:39:25.530 --> 00:39:26.450
Yanked what story?

00:39:27.670 --> 00:39:28.430
Willebrand story.

00:39:29.030 --> 00:39:29.830
On Sally Gardner.

00:39:31.120 --> 00:39:33.600
Well, it was pulled by our advertising department.

00:39:35.080 --> 00:39:39.360
It seems Mr. Andrew Wharton, president of Wharton's department store,

00:39:39.440 --> 00:39:40.560
was Sally's caviar ticket.

00:39:42.840 --> 00:39:45.180
Right, right.

00:39:46.900 --> 00:39:47.460
Posing room.

00:39:48.180 --> 00:39:49.880
One column head, one bank on this weather report.

00:39:51.080 --> 00:39:53.970
Jake, I'm Willebrand's story, Sally Gardner.

00:39:55.020 --> 00:39:57.850
Yeah, hold it, but don't kill it.

00:40:10.140 --> 00:40:12.360
Worry about every story in our paper, Mr. Fenway?

00:40:12.500 --> 00:40:14.140
It just seemed to me this was libelous material.

00:40:14.360 --> 00:40:16.180
We've got over a hundred stories in this issue.

00:40:16.780 --> 00:40:17.720
Check them all for libel?

00:40:17.800 --> 00:40:18.060
No, sir.

00:40:18.100 --> 00:40:18.640
Or any of them?

00:40:18.680 --> 00:40:19.000
No, sir.

00:40:19.160 --> 00:40:19.620
I see.

00:40:20.410 --> 00:40:23.630
You're a self-appointed censor only on stories involving big advertisers.

00:40:23.770 --> 00:40:24.830
I was trying to protect us.

00:40:24.910 --> 00:40:26.830
Us, or you, or Mr. Wharton, and for how much?

00:40:26.910 --> 00:40:27.870
He denies this story.

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:29.330
Willowbrand included his denial.

00:40:30.580 --> 00:40:32.460
I thought as a matter of policy that...

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:32.720
Policy?

00:40:33.540 --> 00:40:35.640
Since when has the advertising department of this paper

00:40:35.640 --> 00:40:37.180
dictated its policy on news?

00:40:37.420 --> 00:40:38.640
I didn't act on my own.

00:40:38.820 --> 00:40:40.000
No, you haven't got the guts.

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:42.730
So you went to Wharton. Run the story.

00:40:42.970 --> 00:40:44.170
I talked to Alice Garrison.

00:40:44.370 --> 00:40:46.570
Mr. Wharton came here. We phoned her. It was on her authority.

00:40:46.650 --> 00:40:48.950
She hasn't got the authority. Not until I'm out of here.

00:40:49.880 --> 00:40:51.340
Well, have you arranged for that, too?

00:40:52.040 --> 00:40:53.630
No, sir. It was slipping.

00:40:55.030 --> 00:40:57.190
Will you talk to Mr. Wharton? He's waiting right in here.

00:41:00.800 --> 00:41:01.720
This is Mr. Hutchison.

00:41:01.980 --> 00:41:02.740
How do you do, sir?

00:41:05.980 --> 00:41:07.260
May I give you my side of it?

00:41:07.860 --> 00:41:08.760
I'll take care of this.

00:41:12.420 --> 00:41:13.840
Please don't publish that story.

00:41:14.140 --> 00:41:15.000
Why? Isn't it true?

00:41:15.200 --> 00:41:18.240
It can't do you much good, and it'll ruin me and hurt my family.

00:41:19.130 --> 00:41:21.390
I've been doing business with your paper for 20 years.

00:41:23.170 --> 00:41:24.790
You're a big advertiser, Mr. Wharton.

00:41:24.870 --> 00:41:26.710
We need your business, but not on those terms.

00:41:26.970 --> 00:41:29.970
All right. I made a mistake with Sally, but that was...

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:33.320
Ten years ago, and I've paid for it in blackmail every month.

00:41:33.480 --> 00:41:35.860
I'm sorry, Mr. Wharton. This is a matter for the police.

00:41:36.120 --> 00:41:37.860
You are interested in facts, aren't you?

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:44.120
One day, Sally phoned, quitting her job at the store.

00:41:44.840 --> 00:41:46.020
She was letting me off the hook.

00:41:47.300 --> 00:41:48.420
She said there was another man.

00:41:49.080 --> 00:41:50.440
Another man there always is.

00:41:50.520 --> 00:41:52.320
In this case, the man was Tomas Rienci.

00:41:53.410 --> 00:41:54.510
Sally said she loved him.

00:41:55.240 --> 00:41:56.380
Wouldn't ever bother me again.

00:41:56.620 --> 00:41:59.610
She even sent back some damaging photographs.

00:42:00.700 --> 00:42:03.080
Of us. She said she was set for life.

00:42:03.580 --> 00:42:04.560
What does she mean by that?

00:42:04.940 --> 00:42:05.580
She didn't say.

00:42:07.960 --> 00:42:11.020
Surely you don't think I'd kill Sally. I haven't seen her in over two years.

00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:14.560
Mrs. Wharton knew about Sally. She suggested that I talk to you.

00:42:14.920 --> 00:42:19.770
Your paper forced me to come here, but now that I'm here, I don't know who...

00:42:20.720 --> 00:42:24.240
Wharton, you can tell your wife I'm holding the story.

00:42:24.870 --> 00:42:25.410
Thank you, sir.

00:42:25.820 --> 00:42:27.560
But a glad yarn about Rienzi doesn't gel.

00:42:27.720 --> 00:42:28.020
It will.

00:42:28.740 --> 00:42:29.920
Better for both eyes' sake.

00:42:32.200 --> 00:42:33.670
Jim. Uh, later, dear.

00:42:34.970 --> 00:42:36.610
What have you got on Sally Gardner?

00:42:37.070 --> 00:42:38.390
The furry blonde? Uh-huh.

00:42:38.780 --> 00:42:40.940
She was abroad. Yeah, but who?

00:42:41.720 --> 00:42:43.140
Since when do you go in for gossip?

00:42:43.610 --> 00:42:52.020
Since now. Jim, do you plan around

00:42:52.020 --> 00:42:53.900
with Sally? What do you want?

00:42:54.690 --> 00:42:55.890
Proof. I want to be sure.

00:42:56.800 --> 00:42:58.680
From this, a fellow could catch a hole in the head.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:01.660
No, he could. That bother you?

00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:03.790
Oh, no! No, no, no.

00:43:14.270 --> 00:43:20.650
Harry, you ever hear of Herman Schmidt? Small-time stuff.

00:43:20.710 --> 00:43:22.630
Had some kind of a political job at the arena.

00:43:22.750 --> 00:43:23.750
Boxing judge, I think.

00:43:24.190 --> 00:43:25.130
Oh, yeah, I got him now.

00:43:25.370 --> 00:43:29.980
Brother of Sally Gardner. Sally may have been tied in with Rianza.

00:43:30.600 --> 00:43:33.620
I know one thing, Rianz. He's tied in with the boxing commission.

00:43:35.650 --> 00:43:38.570
Now, get a hold of Schmidt. Sweat him.

00:43:39.890 --> 00:43:40.590
About his job?

00:43:40.730 --> 00:43:41.350
How about Sally?

00:43:42.520 --> 00:43:44.350
Boy, here's Willebrand's copy.

00:43:44.610 --> 00:43:47.390
All we've got on Sally is she was once a bathing beauty.

00:43:48.120 --> 00:43:49.460
One thing I'm short on is time.

00:43:50.900 --> 00:43:52.600
Sally and her brother were born here.

00:43:53.200 --> 00:43:55.530
Her mother came from Germany, father dead.

00:43:56.280 --> 00:43:57.940
No known criminal record for Sally.

00:43:58.180 --> 00:43:59.480
No recorded marriages.

00:44:02.940 --> 00:44:04.640
Mr. Lewis Schaefer to see you.

00:44:08.300 --> 00:44:13.070
Okay. Bond Dollar.

00:44:13.720 --> 00:44:14.180
Yes, sir.

00:44:14.320 --> 00:44:18.670
Relax. If Lugerman's closed the financial page, ask him to come in.

00:44:19.010 --> 00:44:28.920
Happy to meet you, Mr. Hutchinson.

00:44:30.620 --> 00:44:34.160
All right, you put them up here chronologically.

00:44:34.340 --> 00:44:35.240
Sit down, Mr. Schaefer.

00:44:35.900 --> 00:44:36.780
Thank you, but what I have...

00:44:36.780 --> 00:44:39.660
Give a space for the missing period between Wharton and Rienzi.

00:44:39.780 --> 00:44:40.060
Yes, sir.

00:44:41.190 --> 00:44:42.610
Coffee, Mr. Schaefer? Sandwich?

00:44:42.770 --> 00:44:45.000
No, thanks. I didn't mean to interrupt your work.

00:44:46.560 --> 00:44:47.340
How's my wife?

00:44:47.780 --> 00:44:48.760
What's what I came to see you about?

00:44:48.960 --> 00:44:49.180
Shoot.

00:44:50.750 --> 00:44:51.110
Well...

00:44:51.200 --> 00:44:52.180
Nora asked you to come?

00:44:52.420 --> 00:44:54.930
Well, of course not. I thought that we could...

00:44:56.170 --> 00:44:57.910
Well, this is rather personal, Mr. Hutchison.

00:44:58.580 --> 00:44:59.980
Inasmuch as it concerns my wife...

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:01.680
I hope it's not too personal.

00:45:02.810 --> 00:45:04.290
You're making her very unhappy.

00:45:04.490 --> 00:45:04.830
Want me?

00:45:05.660 --> 00:45:06.120
Oh, look a minute.

00:45:06.400 --> 00:45:08.620
I want to report on all Rianzi's investments,

00:45:08.860 --> 00:45:11.100
legit and otherwise, dummy corporations, everything,

00:45:11.240 --> 00:45:12.920
real estate, manufacturing, investments,

00:45:13.530 --> 00:45:14.430
whatever you can dig up.

00:45:14.590 --> 00:45:15.450
You got a starting point?

00:45:15.950 --> 00:45:16.910
Try the tax reports.

00:45:17.030 --> 00:45:18.790
Charlie and the governor's office might give you a hand.

00:45:18.910 --> 00:45:19.010
Right.

00:45:19.230 --> 00:45:19.830
Make it thorough.

00:45:24.330 --> 00:45:27.280
I'm making her unhappy, Mr. Schaefer.

00:45:27.540 --> 00:45:28.220
Let her alone.

00:45:28.710 --> 00:45:29.970
You're confusing her, making her...

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:32.080
I feel guilty. Her responsibility to you is over.

00:45:32.340 --> 00:45:33.340
Then why are you here?

00:45:33.870 --> 00:45:35.310
I'm only trying to do what's best for Nora.

00:45:36.060 --> 00:45:38.920
That's not only ridiculous, but insulting. You're not that much of a pride.

00:45:39.200 --> 00:45:40.020
Ed, here's the...

00:45:40.080 --> 00:45:41.200
Oh, excuse me.

00:45:43.080 --> 00:45:43.720
What have you got?

00:45:43.920 --> 00:45:45.280
Sally bought some government bonds.

00:45:45.380 --> 00:45:45.660
When?

00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:46.740
Five months ago.

00:45:46.860 --> 00:45:47.840
Sally or Rienzi?

00:45:47.960 --> 00:45:48.580
In her name.

00:45:48.960 --> 00:45:49.800
$40,000 worth.

00:45:50.130 --> 00:45:53.870
And it took some doing it this hour, but we've got a checkup working in every bank for savings accounts.

00:45:54.010 --> 00:45:55.010
Safety deposit boxes?

00:45:55.170 --> 00:45:55.590
That, too.

00:45:55.810 --> 00:45:56.530
That's all we've got.

00:45:56.980 --> 00:45:58.050
Okay. Let's hear, Frank.

00:46:00.190 --> 00:46:03.190
Sally's a high school girl, model for Wharton's department store,

00:46:03.370 --> 00:46:06.990
showgirl, kept girl, missing portion of the river.

00:46:07.690 --> 00:46:10.550
Now, if we can plug up this hole between Wharton's department store and the river,

00:46:10.650 --> 00:46:11.690
fill it up with Rianzi.

00:46:14.280 --> 00:46:16.240
Goodbye, Mr. Schaefer. I can't wish you good luck.

00:46:16.510 --> 00:46:17.270
You know how it is.

00:46:17.520 --> 00:46:19.360
There's something you ought to know, Mr. Hutchison.

00:46:19.460 --> 00:46:19.640
Yes?

00:46:20.030 --> 00:46:21.150
Jim Cleary on one.

00:46:22.190 --> 00:46:22.630
Yes, Jim.

00:46:23.350 --> 00:46:25.310
Nora and I are getting married tomorrow night.

00:46:29.220 --> 00:46:29.980
I thought it best not...

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:32.480
Not to delay any longer. You know how it is.

00:46:36.120 --> 00:46:37.040
Yes, yes, go ahead.

00:46:37.360 --> 00:46:39.200
Here it is. Just what you've been looking for.

00:46:40.520 --> 00:46:42.740
Yeah. Rienzi's your boy, all right.

00:46:48.580 --> 00:46:48.800
Frank.

00:46:49.120 --> 00:46:50.420
Oh, it's the only show Rienzi backed.

00:46:51.010 --> 00:46:52.310
He insisted Sally be in it.

00:46:53.560 --> 00:46:54.660
I'm with Al Murray now.

00:46:56.190 --> 00:46:59.970
He says Rienzi used to send a car around for her every night after the show.

00:47:00.090 --> 00:47:03.360
Holden, quit this call to rewrite. You hear that, Cleary?

00:47:03.930 --> 00:47:09.230
Okay. Oh, Frank, on the Willebrandt story,

00:47:09.780 --> 00:47:12.780
kill the part about Wharton, use Cleary's story for a lead-all.

00:47:13.300 --> 00:47:15.360
Throw in Sally's face. No nudity.

00:47:22.140 --> 00:47:22.640
Bond Dollar.

00:47:23.270 --> 00:47:26.680
I want a complete check on Lewis Schaefer,

00:47:26.840 --> 00:47:28.960
who runs the United Advertising Agency.

00:47:29.700 --> 00:47:29.960
Schaefer...

00:47:32.840 --> 00:47:35.080
Anything from Thompson yet?

00:47:35.310 --> 00:47:35.610
No.

00:47:49.520 --> 00:47:57.520
Mr. Schmidt? Mr. Schmidt?

00:48:06.108 --> 00:48:06.568
Don't move.

00:48:07.068 --> 00:48:07.688
I just want to...

00:48:07.688 --> 00:48:08.048
Shut up.

00:48:14.044 --> 00:48:16.504
your head off

00:48:19.740 --> 00:48:20.580
Sit down.

00:48:21.308 --> 00:48:22.148
No, look at it.

00:48:27.228 --> 00:48:28.228
things on the table.

00:48:32.684 --> 00:48:34.704
Relax, Herman. I'm here to help you.

00:48:35.436 --> 00:48:36.276
Who's with it?

00:48:36.652 --> 00:48:36.992
Nobody.

00:48:39.724 --> 00:48:40.564
What's the pitch?

00:48:41.772 --> 00:48:43.852
My name is Thompson, reporter, sports.

00:48:44.300 --> 00:48:45.680
For the day of... Don't do that!

00:48:49.164 --> 00:48:49.664
Sports, huh?

00:48:54.156 --> 00:48:54.756
What'd you write today?

00:49:01.564 --> 00:49:04.784
The question of televising next season's baseball games

00:49:04.784 --> 00:49:08.704
was discussed at a heated session of the Hot Stove League yesterday.

00:49:13.948 --> 00:49:14.948
What do you want?

00:49:16.060 --> 00:49:18.060
Do you mind putting that thing away?

00:49:19.068 --> 00:49:19.988
How did you find me?

00:49:20.828 --> 00:49:23.448
You know a lot of people in the fight game. They owe me favors.

00:49:24.092 --> 00:49:24.992
I collected a few.

00:49:26.524 --> 00:49:27.484
All right, get to it.

00:49:31.980 --> 00:49:33.460
Why did Rienzi...

00:49:33.548 --> 00:49:34.428
kill your sister.

00:49:36.748 --> 00:49:37.028
Did he?

00:49:40.108 --> 00:49:40.888
Who were you afraid of?

00:49:42.668 --> 00:49:43.388
Why the hideout?

00:49:45.420 --> 00:49:46.220
That won't get you anywhere.

00:49:47.308 --> 00:49:49.548
I phoned your address into the paper. They know I'm here.

00:49:50.732 --> 00:49:52.892
By the next edition, Rienzi will know where you are.

00:49:58.540 --> 00:49:59.480
Your only chance, Herman.

00:50:00.828 --> 00:50:02.608
Let me take you to the paper and you'll be safe.

00:50:05.372 --> 00:50:09.032
Sooner or later, Rienzi will get to you and you'll wind up in the morgue beside Sally.

00:50:13.468 --> 00:50:16.468
As long as Rienzi is free, you're a dead pigeon.

00:50:20.124 --> 00:50:21.124
Take your time.

00:50:22.556 --> 00:50:25.796
Rienzi won't get his copy of the day with your address until morning.

00:50:43.148 --> 00:50:46.908
I have carefully read the last will and testament of the deceased, John Garrison.

00:50:47.660 --> 00:50:52.400
I find nothing therein to prevent the sale of the publication enterprises known as the day.

00:50:52.972 --> 00:50:53.512
Your Honour.

00:50:53.804 --> 00:50:57.624
Mrs. Garrison, wife of the deceased and one of the heirs, would like to address the court.

00:50:58.444 --> 00:50:59.284
Mrs. Garrison?

00:51:01.564 --> 00:51:04.244
Sir, I object to the sale of this paper to Mr. White.

00:51:04.700 --> 00:51:06.540
Your Honor, the business garrison is agreed.

00:51:07.164 --> 00:51:10.624
But this request for sale was signed by you, Mrs. Garrison.

00:51:10.780 --> 00:51:11.900
I've changed my mind.

00:51:12.080 --> 00:51:14.760
Mrs. Garrison's daughters have not, and they constitute a majority.

00:51:15.324 --> 00:51:18.624
My husband would not have wished this paper to be sold to Mr. White.

00:51:18.908 --> 00:51:19.908
How do you know?

00:51:19.996 --> 00:51:24.036
Your Honor, I object to cross-examination until the witness has completed her statement.

00:51:24.732 --> 00:51:26.572
You knew the paper was being sold to Mr. White.

00:51:27.132 --> 00:51:29.312
but I did not know it was going to be rubbed out of existence.

00:51:30.060 --> 00:51:32.510
Which it will be if this contract is approved.

00:51:33.020 --> 00:51:36.940
What happens to this newspaper after it has been sold is of absolutely no concern here.

00:51:37.330 --> 00:51:39.370
Is Mr. Crane the lawyer and the judge?

00:51:39.790 --> 00:51:41.930
This is a cheap display of sensationalism and conspiracy.

00:51:42.410 --> 00:51:44.590
Would the other heirs care to reconsider?

00:51:45.000 --> 00:51:45.710
No, sir.

00:51:45.830 --> 00:51:50.260
Would it be all right, Mr. Crane, if they answered for themselves?

00:51:54.750 --> 00:51:58.990
Mrs. Alice Garrison Courtney, do you still wish to sell?

00:52:02.000 --> 00:52:05.390
Mrs. Catherine Garrison Geary.

00:52:06.860 --> 00:52:07.400
Yes, sir.

00:52:10.930 --> 00:52:13.030
In that case, the paper may be sold.

00:52:13.420 --> 00:52:14.180
Then I'll buy it.

00:52:14.300 --> 00:52:15.720
The contract already exists.

00:52:15.900 --> 00:52:18.420
But, Your Honor, Mrs. Garrison has priority of purchase.

00:52:18.690 --> 00:52:20.210
I'll raise Mr. White's offer.

00:52:20.630 --> 00:52:22.870
Your Honor, I cannot see that my client's interest...

00:52:22.870 --> 00:52:24.950
Why don't you object to my daughter's receiving more money?

00:52:25.090 --> 00:52:27.070
That's what they're selling out for, isn't it? Money?

00:52:27.270 --> 00:52:29.130
Well, counsel kindly step up here.

00:52:34.730 --> 00:52:36.670
I can, I want to, and I'm going to.

00:52:36.830 --> 00:52:37.690
What good will it do?

00:52:38.820 --> 00:52:41.340
You'll be happy to know this stupidity is not hereditary.

00:52:41.580 --> 00:52:42.900
You've acquired it all by yourselves.

00:52:43.240 --> 00:52:44.780
You're making us sound like fools.

00:52:45.760 --> 00:52:47.800
Well, what changed your mind?

00:52:48.800 --> 00:52:51.160
Have you seen today's paper and yesterday's?

00:52:51.980 --> 00:52:53.360
Loyalty changed my mind.

00:52:54.120 --> 00:52:56.380
The principle evidently lacking in the present generation.

00:52:57.530 --> 00:52:59.050
You haven't got the money to buy the paper.

00:52:59.340 --> 00:52:59.960
I'll get it.

00:53:00.380 --> 00:53:01.180
You're crazy.

00:53:02.790 --> 00:53:03.950
No, just ashamed.

00:53:04.360 --> 00:53:06.880
Ashamed for me, for you, and for your father.

00:53:07.760 --> 00:53:09.800
I'm not going to let this paper die.

00:53:11.020 --> 00:53:12.180
That makes me crazy.

00:53:13.480 --> 00:53:14.780
I'm good and crazy.

00:53:15.850 --> 00:53:16.170
All right.

00:53:20.060 --> 00:53:23.560
I shall require time to consider Mrs. Garrison's request.

00:53:24.830 --> 00:53:27.790
Counsel will be notified when this court will reconvene.

00:53:28.040 --> 00:53:28.400
Sir.

00:53:29.060 --> 00:53:29.620
Mr. White.

00:53:30.120 --> 00:53:36.660
any delay even 24 hours will wreck the value of the day people will not buy a dying paper nor

00:53:36.660 --> 00:53:42.940
advertise in it now the staff of the day will become demoralized no newspaper can function under

00:53:42.940 --> 00:53:53.630
this handicap thank you mr white however i must preserve decision but if the current high standard

00:53:53.630 --> 00:53:59.980
of journalism in the days slackens or any act of neglect threatens the well-being of this newspaper

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:05.570
I shall be forced to make an immediate decision based upon the current contract.

00:54:12.360 --> 00:54:13.680
Dr. Hutchison, my name is Hanson.

00:54:13.820 --> 00:54:14.100
Yes?

00:54:14.480 --> 00:54:18.050
I'm Mr. Yancey's lawyer. He's waiting to see you in his car.

00:54:18.470 --> 00:54:18.790
Why?

00:54:19.280 --> 00:54:20.120
Personal business.

00:54:21.760 --> 00:54:22.400
A ride?

00:54:23.090 --> 00:54:23.970
No, sir, a drive.

00:54:31.980 --> 00:54:41.810
How do you do, Mr. Hutchison? Give you a lift some place?

00:54:42.150 --> 00:54:44.500
Why? Sociable type.

00:54:45.130 --> 00:54:46.790
They're expecting me at my office.

00:54:48.980 --> 00:54:49.660
Okay, Lippin.

00:54:50.650 --> 00:54:51.230
Okay what?

00:54:52.080 --> 00:54:52.740
Just okay.

00:54:53.540 --> 00:54:56.080
I wondered when and how you'd get around to this.

00:54:56.380 --> 00:54:56.600
Yeah?

00:54:57.360 --> 00:54:59.280
Yeah, I expected something a little more poetic.

00:55:00.920 --> 00:55:09.530
drink oh now that's rather poetic will it be nothing not a drinking man not in an armored

00:55:09.530 --> 00:55:19.480
car i think i like you why imagination i like a man with imagination you're a good newspaper man

00:55:19.480 --> 00:55:24.790
they say you're not bad at your trade either you got two full surprises they say are they

00:55:24.790 --> 00:55:29.980
Wait much cash about five hundred dollars a piece your kind of imagination is

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:31.100
I agree.

00:55:32.040 --> 00:55:33.980
But you're a hothead, they say.

00:55:34.160 --> 00:55:34.680
Who's they?

00:55:35.480 --> 00:55:35.920
Friends.

00:55:37.290 --> 00:55:38.470
I got friends everywhere.

00:55:39.800 --> 00:55:41.220
I'd like for you to be my friend.

00:55:41.580 --> 00:55:42.420
I got a friend.

00:55:42.560 --> 00:55:43.280
Not like me.

00:55:43.900 --> 00:55:45.420
Is that a proposal or a proposition?

00:55:46.820 --> 00:55:47.860
What do you got against me?

00:55:49.100 --> 00:55:49.900
You're not my type.

00:55:50.620 --> 00:55:51.660
You ever meet me before?

00:55:52.770 --> 00:55:53.770
Do business with me?

00:55:54.830 --> 00:55:56.410
Maybe you got the wrong impression of me.

00:55:56.590 --> 00:55:58.850
What kind of an impression would you like me to have?

00:56:02.044 --> 00:56:03.584
My family reads a paper.

00:56:05.276 --> 00:56:06.936
It's not nice what you print.

00:56:08.188 --> 00:56:09.228
I got a nice family.

00:56:10.364 --> 00:56:11.944
Sometime I'd like for you to meet them.

00:56:12.732 --> 00:56:15.932
No point in that, unless they're the ones that almost killed Burroughs.

00:56:16.508 --> 00:56:17.048
Burrows.

00:56:17.372 --> 00:56:19.432
Never beat up a reporter, they say.

00:56:19.712 --> 00:56:21.712
It's like killing a cop on duty, they say.

00:56:22.716 --> 00:56:26.136
Never drop girls in the river, clothed or unclothed, they say.

00:56:26.460 --> 00:56:28.660
What have I got to do with reporters or girls?

00:56:29.020 --> 00:56:29.980
I'm in a cementin' cunt.

00:56:30.940 --> 00:56:32.520
Capone was in the insurance business.

00:56:32.760 --> 00:56:34.160
You've got a sense of humor, friend.

00:56:34.570 --> 00:56:35.490
Why don't you laugh?

00:56:35.670 --> 00:56:36.350
Very funny.

00:56:36.850 --> 00:56:38.790
Tomorrow's newspaper will be even funnier.

00:56:44.580 --> 00:56:46.500
That's the Rienzi I like to see.

00:56:47.420 --> 00:56:48.580
This way you start shooting.

00:56:48.700 --> 00:56:50.480
What are you supposed to be, a little tin guard?

00:56:51.120 --> 00:56:52.960
You gonna save the world, a hero or something?

00:56:54.020 --> 00:56:55.660
There's only one kind of martyr, friend.

00:56:56.300 --> 00:56:56.760
Dead ones.

00:56:57.300 --> 00:56:59.740
Show me a martyr, I'll lay you four to one he winds up out of the money.

00:57:01.116 --> 00:57:02.696
My lawyer says I can sue you for this.

00:57:03.932 --> 00:57:04.432
Well...

00:57:05.372 --> 00:57:07.252
What you're trying to do has been tried before.

00:57:08.060 --> 00:57:09.560
Nobody could ever make it stick.

00:57:10.812 --> 00:57:12.992
In that case, you got nothing to worry about.

00:57:21.500 --> 00:57:22.460
Thanks for the lift.

00:57:23.260 --> 00:57:24.680
I can't say I enjoyed it.

00:57:24.828 --> 00:57:25.328
Cops.

00:57:25.564 --> 00:57:28.864
Tax collectors, politicians, citizens' committees, they all got an angle.

00:57:29.276 --> 00:57:29.916
What's yours?

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:31.080
Just name it. What do you want?

00:57:31.610 --> 00:57:33.510
My prizes await more than Pulitzer's.

00:57:33.730 --> 00:57:36.550
I know. I gotta look at Sally's fur coat.

00:57:42.720 --> 00:57:44.460
Wasn't that Hermit Schmidt just went in?

00:57:45.470 --> 00:57:57.790
Yeah. Okay.

00:58:00.360 --> 00:58:08.530
First, see the money. Said you'd pay for the story. Five grand.

00:58:08.750 --> 00:58:14.070
One thousand dollars.

00:58:14.550 --> 00:58:14.970
How far would you go?

00:58:15.000 --> 00:58:18.620
Out of the country after you testify against Rienzi, yes or no?

00:58:18.880 --> 00:58:21.100
They said you'd protect me.

00:58:21.480 --> 00:58:24.460
These days, accommodations in jail are hard to get.

00:58:24.560 --> 00:58:26.260
However, I'll use my influence.

00:58:26.640 --> 00:58:27.160
Well?

00:58:30.760 --> 00:58:35.240
All right. Get some sleep, and on your way out, have them send in $1,000.

00:58:35.880 --> 00:58:36.600
In cash.

00:58:37.080 --> 00:58:37.660
In cash.

00:58:41.490 --> 00:58:43.230
Okay. You've got the floor.

00:58:46.690 --> 00:58:49.170
You want me to start?

00:58:50.860 --> 00:58:51.840
Sally and Rienzi.

00:58:51.900 --> 00:58:54.230
Well, they liked each other.

00:58:54.750 --> 00:58:55.110
Liked?

00:58:55.190 --> 00:58:57.080
You know.

00:58:57.640 --> 00:58:58.640
Rienzi pay her bills?

00:59:00.860 --> 00:59:01.160
What else?

00:59:01.360 --> 00:59:02.240
For everything.

00:59:02.480 --> 00:59:05.040
It was worth it.

00:59:05.060 --> 00:59:06.370
That's what I like.

00:59:06.620 --> 00:59:07.460
Family pride.

00:59:07.820 --> 00:59:09.500
Harry Enfrey kicked in for the apartment.

00:59:09.660 --> 00:59:11.320
Her fur coat, some cheap jewelry.

00:59:11.520 --> 00:59:12.850
Maybe her car, too.

00:59:13.070 --> 00:59:14.980
But Sally bought...

00:59:15.000 --> 00:59:18.580
40,000 dollars worth of government bonds in her name.

00:59:19.540 --> 00:59:20.600
Rienzi pay for that?

00:59:22.600 --> 00:59:23.060
Yes, sir.

00:59:23.200 --> 00:59:24.230
You're a liar.

00:59:25.240 --> 00:59:28.480
Sally used the 200,000 dollars Rienzi gave her to hold for him.

00:59:28.560 --> 00:59:29.800
Why 200,000 dollars?

00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:32.710
Why would a guy par with that kind of scratch? Hot money.

00:59:33.100 --> 00:59:35.340
The city bank says your sister rented a safety deposit box.

00:59:35.480 --> 00:59:36.480
She gave it up a month ago.

00:59:36.620 --> 00:59:38.080
On the same day she moved out of her apartment.

00:59:38.260 --> 00:59:38.500
Why?

00:59:42.380 --> 00:59:44.830
All right, it's true. He gave her the money to keep for her.

00:59:45.890 --> 00:59:48.330
When he wanted it back, she was scared he'd make a break.

00:59:49.450 --> 00:59:56.850
She said as long as she kept the cash, that couldn't work out that way.

01:00:01.600 --> 01:00:02.540
I don't feel so good.

01:00:10.830 --> 01:00:12.070
Have this tight. More coming.

01:00:12.270 --> 01:00:14.190
Yes, Miss Garrison wants to see you in the dome.

01:00:14.430 --> 01:00:14.990
Oh, stall her.

01:00:15.090 --> 01:00:16.370
Here's that $1,000 you wanted.

01:00:16.670 --> 01:00:16.930
Uh-huh.

01:00:20.290 --> 01:00:22.230
Oh, Al, get your camera in there.

01:00:22.760 --> 01:00:24.190
Are you married, honey?

01:00:24.830 --> 01:00:25.230
Bless it.

01:00:25.430 --> 01:00:26.410
Later, baby.

01:00:27.340 --> 01:00:28.200
Get a hold of the governor.

01:00:28.850 --> 01:00:29.970
Ask him if he'll appoint a special...

01:00:30.090 --> 01:00:32.050
We've got a grand jury to investigate the last election.

01:00:32.380 --> 01:00:33.480
Some of the names will come up.

01:00:34.420 --> 01:00:35.860
We supported a few for office.

01:00:36.040 --> 01:00:37.580
This paper has no political party.

01:00:37.700 --> 01:00:39.760
We support men for office, some good, some bad.

01:00:40.040 --> 01:00:41.480
Mr. Hutchison, Mr. Willebrand's on here.

01:00:42.630 --> 01:00:45.670
If the governor won't act, get the chairman of the state senate committee.

01:00:47.150 --> 01:00:47.450
Yes?

01:00:48.080 --> 01:00:48.480
Hutchison.

01:00:49.000 --> 01:00:51.780
Oh, hold it.

01:00:53.080 --> 01:00:53.900
Go ahead, Willebrand.

01:00:56.740 --> 01:00:57.180
Shall we?

01:00:58.030 --> 01:00:58.610
What about it?

01:01:00.600 --> 01:01:03.830
Where'd she move to? What hotel?

01:01:05.010 --> 01:01:06.370
When did you find out Sally was dead?

01:01:06.810 --> 01:01:08.860
Well, I read about it in the paper.

01:01:09.140 --> 01:01:10.740
She was dead three days before the papers got it.

01:01:10.940 --> 01:01:13.020
Your mother says you left the house last Saturday and didn't come back.

01:01:13.240 --> 01:01:14.320
Sally was killed that same night.

01:01:14.440 --> 01:01:16.840
So what? I leave the house lots of times for weeks sometimes.

01:01:17.000 --> 01:01:19.590
But not to hide out. You were afraid of Rienzi. Why?

01:01:20.280 --> 01:01:21.900
You knew he was going to Sally's place last Saturday.

01:01:22.000 --> 01:01:23.620
I didn't even know where she lived.

01:01:23.760 --> 01:01:25.050
Hold it. Shoot.

01:01:27.780 --> 01:01:29.980
Sally was moved from her apartment on Maple Avenue.

01:01:30.000 --> 01:01:33.360
for the inner-city storage company four weeks ago.

01:01:34.370 --> 01:01:38.050
The Leroy Hotel, registered under the name of Bessie Schmidt.

01:01:38.900 --> 01:01:42.240
Never left her room. She had only two visitors, your mother and you.

01:01:42.890 --> 01:01:44.350
You were there Saturday night.

01:01:45.480 --> 01:01:46.760
Well, I don't remember. Maybe I was.

01:01:46.900 --> 01:01:47.760
Why did you go there?

01:01:48.390 --> 01:01:53.020
Well, I, uh, she phoned me.

01:01:53.770 --> 01:01:55.170
Yeah, that's right. She phoned me.

01:01:55.290 --> 01:01:58.730
The desk clerk says you phoned her from the desk, 1.30 a.m.

01:01:58.970 --> 01:01:59.710
He could be wrong.

01:02:05.020 --> 01:02:08.640
That's right. Somewhere out there, Rienzi's waiting for you.

01:02:10.940 --> 01:02:12.680
Either you tell the truth or I'll turn you loose.

01:02:12.820 --> 01:02:13.820
No money, no protection.

01:02:20.040 --> 01:02:25.950
Wait, Rienzi wanted his money.

01:02:27.830 --> 01:02:29.050
They couldn't find out where she was living.

01:02:29.390 --> 01:02:29.970
So you...

01:02:30.580 --> 01:02:32.530
Why'd you do it? What did Rienzi promise?

01:02:32.790 --> 01:02:36.960
Well, he got me my job. I owed him some money. I couldn't pay him.

01:02:38.550 --> 01:02:39.890
He said the favor would square us.

01:02:41.230 --> 01:02:43.610
All you had to do was put the finger on your own sister.

01:02:43.810 --> 01:02:45.690
I didn't know what they were gonna do, I swear.

01:02:46.660 --> 01:02:48.500
Who went with you, Rienzi? I went along.

01:02:48.680 --> 01:02:50.080
So she wouldn't be afraid to let you in?

01:02:50.280 --> 01:02:50.960
They came later.

01:02:51.730 --> 01:02:53.770
All you did was open the door for them, that's all.

01:02:53.850 --> 01:02:54.370
Who's they?

01:02:55.000 --> 01:02:56.980
Lefty Smith, Whitey Franks, and Kid Jones.

01:02:57.180 --> 01:02:58.080
They belong to Rienzi?

01:02:58.360 --> 01:02:59.700
Except Whitey, he hides out.

01:03:01.590 --> 01:03:14.890
Well, they asked her for the money. She wouldn't give, so Whitey, he hit her, then Lefty, she began to scream, hollered for me to help her.

01:03:17.620 --> 01:03:22.700
Whitey, shut her up. I got scared.

01:03:23.960 --> 01:03:29.220
I couldn't watch what they were doing when I ran into the bathroom, beat it out of there.

01:03:30.960 --> 01:03:32.640
That's all I know. Honest.

01:03:33.160 --> 01:03:36.080
Yes. Mrs. Garrison's still waiting, one left the other.

01:03:37.080 --> 01:03:41.380
I'll be right up. Word was out. Rienzi wanted me.

01:03:43.060 --> 01:03:44.160
He was afraid I was gonna sing.

01:03:45.420 --> 01:03:46.980
If I stay for the trial, they'll get to me.

01:03:47.640 --> 01:03:50.330
You don't know them. In jail. No matter where or anywhere.

01:03:50.350 --> 01:03:51.330
If that's typed, have them sign it.

01:03:51.520 --> 01:03:53.300
We won't have time to get this all in the Bulldog.

01:03:53.520 --> 01:03:55.140
We'll get the text to the statement in the second edition.

01:03:55.430 --> 01:03:57.110
Count it. Count it.

01:03:58.830 --> 01:03:59.970
Have this office. Fume it.

01:04:09.920 --> 01:04:12.000
Mr. Hutchinson, about Lewis Schaefer.

01:04:12.140 --> 01:04:13.980
On that information you requested.

01:04:14.500 --> 01:04:17.000
Lewis Schaefer, age 42, four in Baltimore,

01:04:17.380 --> 01:04:19.840
only child of John and Harriet Schaefer of a chemical fortune.

01:04:21.600 --> 01:04:22.620
Was he ever married before?

01:04:22.880 --> 01:04:23.400
No, sir.

01:04:23.580 --> 01:04:26.050
Ever get pinched? Was he ever arrested?

01:04:26.290 --> 01:04:27.490
No record, if he was.

01:04:27.790 --> 01:04:29.190
Alcoholic? Swindler?

01:04:30.570 --> 01:04:32.690
Maybe he's a fiend. You know, he looked like one.

01:04:33.780 --> 01:04:36.300
Check his army record. Maybe he's a spy.

01:04:36.520 --> 01:04:38.180
Got the silver star and the purple heart.

01:04:40.490 --> 01:04:41.590
It's a rotten report.

01:04:42.290 --> 01:04:42.910
Yes, sir.

01:04:43.650 --> 01:04:45.280
Eddie, two things.

01:04:46.020 --> 01:04:47.420
Rienzi started his libel suit.

01:04:47.600 --> 01:04:49.460
He was over the papers half an hour ago.

01:04:49.910 --> 01:04:50.230
Second.

01:04:50.900 --> 01:04:53.360
Judge McKay is going to hand down his decision tonight at 9.

01:04:53.560 --> 01:04:55.760
Because the libel suit? We'll be ready for him.

01:04:55.940 --> 01:04:56.560
And another thing.

01:04:57.020 --> 01:04:59.440
That 1% you were promised when we sold the paper.

01:05:00.030 --> 01:05:01.850
Well, Alice and Kitty have withdrawn it.

01:05:02.400 --> 01:05:03.620
What took them so long?

01:05:04.080 --> 01:05:06.030
You were wonderful today, baby.

01:05:06.670 --> 01:05:08.650
Mr. Blake and Mr. Green, this is Mr. Hutchinson.

01:05:08.730 --> 01:05:08.850
How do you do?

01:05:09.390 --> 01:05:10.150
How do you do, Mr. Green?

01:05:10.170 --> 01:05:14.530
Their banking firm has offered to lend us the extra money to meet Mr. White's offer.

01:05:14.860 --> 01:05:16.120
Pending a few packs, of course.

01:05:16.360 --> 01:05:16.620
Of course.

01:05:17.340 --> 01:05:23.080
Try to remember Rienzi's exact words when he asked you to bring the three hoods to Sally's hotel room.

01:05:23.380 --> 01:05:24.420
We can get that...

01:05:25.460 --> 01:05:27.120
We've got a warrant here for Herman Schmidt.

01:05:28.870 --> 01:05:29.970
We're not finished yet. We want him to...

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:31.600
I just signed a statement. It'll only take a few minutes.

01:05:31.820 --> 01:05:33.900
Come along, Schmidt. You got no right to take statements.

01:05:33.980 --> 01:05:35.360
As long as it's not a police state we have.

01:05:35.600 --> 01:05:35.740
Sorry.

01:05:40.150 --> 01:05:40.510
Whitey.

01:05:41.050 --> 01:05:41.430
Shut up.

01:05:43.840 --> 01:05:45.720
I didn't tell him anything.

01:05:45.900 --> 01:05:50.880
I didn't tell him anything.

01:05:54.130 --> 01:05:56.350
That doesn't include the higher cost of newsprint.

01:05:56.920 --> 01:05:57.360
How's that?

01:05:57.960 --> 01:05:59.850
It keeps going up. Right now it's $110.

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:03.000
$10 a ton. In 1942, it was $50 a ton.

01:06:03.250 --> 01:06:06.850
It takes talent to get the news, think it through, write it, and back it up with research.

01:06:07.580 --> 01:06:09.540
Without good reporting, you haven't got a paper.

01:06:10.410 --> 01:06:12.870
That extra 4% might make it a dangerous venture.

01:06:14.460 --> 01:06:17.280
Free press, sir, like a free life, is always in danger.

01:07:04.440 --> 01:07:08.350
I like the proposition. Barring unforeseen complications, I think...

01:07:08.350 --> 01:07:11.960
And it's you. Yeah?

01:07:37.420 --> 01:07:40.060
Give him a description of what that so-called police look like.

01:07:40.180 --> 01:07:40.740
Yes, Captain.

01:07:43.370 --> 01:07:45.690
When's the press gonna grow up and stop playing detective?

01:07:46.970 --> 01:07:48.690
Can't you tell the difference between a hoodlum and a cop?

01:07:49.170 --> 01:07:49.770
In this town?

01:07:50.970 --> 01:07:51.390
Yes, sir.

01:07:51.900 --> 01:07:56.540
The address?

01:07:58.280 --> 01:07:59.840
See the Mr. Garrison gets home, all right.

01:08:00.120 --> 01:08:01.700
What about Schmitz's confession? Do we run anyway?

01:08:02.100 --> 01:08:05.920
Without his signature, the judge would surely close us down.

01:08:08.490 --> 01:08:12.480
You made a mess of it. I told you I don't want no violence.

01:08:13.250 --> 01:08:14.130
Not yet, anyway.

01:08:15.230 --> 01:08:17.050
There's a time and place for this kind of thing.

01:08:17.530 --> 01:08:18.630
There's a stupid...

01:08:21.020 --> 01:08:24.400
No, no, run away from what?

01:08:25.180 --> 01:08:27.140
I'll talk to them myself, personally.

01:08:28.060 --> 01:08:29.980
Get them down here to my office, all of them.

01:08:31.240 --> 01:08:33.940
Sure, now, right now, you too.

01:08:35.330 --> 01:08:37.790
Find Sally's old lady, Mrs. Schmidt, bring her in.

01:08:37.870 --> 01:08:38.690
I want to talk to her.

01:08:38.910 --> 01:08:40.790
And this time, don't foul it up.

01:08:46.190 --> 01:08:49.090
No paper ever did a better, faster, more thorough job.

01:08:49.970 --> 01:08:53.140
All we needed was that one bit of evidence, we had it.

01:08:55.070 --> 01:08:57.430
Why do you think we hung the whole thing on Rienzi's case?

01:08:58.510 --> 01:08:59.970
Because we were sentimental about a dead...

01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:08.370
girl in a mink coat no and we had something big big enough to save our necks rienzi in the liquor

01:09:08.370 --> 01:09:13.180
business the financial department dug that one up distributor for two of the biggest name brands

01:09:13.420 --> 01:09:19.820
rienzi's brother runs a wire service for race results transportation loan agency for bookies

01:09:19.820 --> 01:09:28.350
real estate hotels nightclubs slot machines etc etc years ago my husband tried to do a story like

01:09:28.350 --> 01:09:29.960
this and a man just like

01:09:30.880 --> 01:09:34.100
No, I figured with a story like this to tell, they'd never close this town.

01:09:36.210 --> 01:09:40.910
Well, showed them how a real newspaper can function.

01:09:41.820 --> 01:09:44.130
And now we're licked, baby.

01:09:45.280 --> 01:09:46.580
Put a head on this, will you?

01:09:50.250 --> 01:09:54.400
My husband always said it was a worthwhile fight, didn't matter who won.

01:09:55.240 --> 01:09:56.980
Some good was sure to come out of it.

01:10:00.440 --> 01:10:01.600
That re-ends his wine?

01:10:01.940 --> 01:10:02.400
Uh-huh.

01:10:03.060 --> 01:10:03.600
Pretty good.

01:10:04.700 --> 01:10:05.680
You're the best.

01:10:07.660 --> 01:10:08.940
Now you're quite a girl.

01:10:09.470 --> 01:10:09.690
Yeah.

01:10:11.660 --> 01:10:13.180
If they made them different in your day.

01:10:13.690 --> 01:10:13.950
Yeah.

01:10:14.740 --> 01:10:15.500
More durable.

01:10:16.710 --> 01:10:17.490
More pliable.

01:10:18.470 --> 01:10:21.300
Girls these days have stuff, but they're brittle.

01:10:22.400 --> 01:10:23.280
Break more easily.

01:10:24.090 --> 01:10:25.150
Don't roll with the punches.

01:10:26.410 --> 01:10:28.270
Plenty of gall and no guts.

01:10:30.380 --> 01:10:37.620
meaning nora meaning nora well now be respectful silence while we feel sorry for ourselves

01:10:37.620 --> 01:10:45.620
well she had no right to walk out on me why not because because it inconvenienced you

01:10:45.620 --> 01:10:52.200
because she's my wife you wouldn't have had a wife of that newspaper it had beautiful legs

01:10:54.590 --> 01:10:57.290
You never walked out on John.

01:10:57.660 --> 01:10:58.520
Who said so?

01:10:58.990 --> 01:10:59.370
Twice.

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:05.820
about a pretty good reason the best a bride always likes to think she's indispensable even

01:11:06.940 --> 01:11:14.230
in the morning i woke up and he was gone gone back to the paper to do the lusitania story

01:11:14.230 --> 01:11:22.780
i walked out ah but you came back two days later he didn't even know i'd been gone but he loved

01:11:22.780 --> 01:11:29.120
you passionately between editions he had time to change the face of journalism fight

01:11:30.060 --> 01:11:36.770
for reform crusade for a thousand lost causes we had no time for his family so i took my two

01:11:36.770 --> 01:11:43.220
daughters and left this big beautiful mausoleum why did you come back well we needed each other

01:11:43.220 --> 01:11:51.690
was i who did the adjusting though wasn't alice or kitty or john he needed a son to carry on the

01:11:51.690 --> 01:11:59.710
paper and they needed a father in love not a bulldog edition enter me bit an image

01:12:02.010 --> 01:12:02.930
What did you want?

01:12:03.470 --> 01:12:04.270
To be useful.

01:12:05.580 --> 01:12:08.860
Well, to newspapers.

01:12:10.040 --> 01:12:13.120
To editors like you. Publishers delight.

01:12:17.800 --> 01:12:18.600
Don't blame Nora.

01:12:20.110 --> 01:12:22.650
Unless she wants to come back, it won't work.

01:12:22.850 --> 01:12:27.950
If she stays away, you can look for a newspaper with nice legs.

01:12:29.090 --> 01:12:29.970
Court convenes them.

01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:31.500
About half an hour. Are you gonna be there?

01:12:31.760 --> 01:12:37.220
Maybe. Will you marry me?

01:12:37.460 --> 01:12:38.380
You're too old.

01:12:39.060 --> 01:12:43.620
You go through old Lady Schmidt's house?

01:12:43.740 --> 01:12:44.300
Talk to bottom.

01:12:44.800 --> 01:12:45.540
Well? Nothing.

01:12:46.810 --> 01:12:48.430
Mrs. Schmidt, did you find her? Not yet.

01:12:48.940 --> 01:12:50.360
Got anybody at the house waiting for her?

01:12:50.420 --> 01:12:51.120
Inside and out.

01:12:51.490 --> 01:12:52.410
Don't bring her here.

01:12:53.760 --> 01:12:55.520
I don't want none of your boys around here.

01:12:56.150 --> 01:12:57.710
Now or any time, you understand?

01:12:57.950 --> 01:12:58.290
I understand.

01:12:58.650 --> 01:12:59.750
And don't phone me.

01:13:00.350 --> 01:13:02.560
Here or at home. Any of you.

01:13:02.720 --> 01:13:04.160
Would it be better if we left town for a while?

01:13:04.300 --> 01:13:05.840
No. We stay put.

01:13:05.960 --> 01:13:07.300
Suppose the grand jury indicts.

01:13:07.320 --> 01:13:09.620
Leave them indicts. Larry will take care of things.

01:13:09.930 --> 01:13:10.550
I'll try.

01:13:11.100 --> 01:13:12.680
That's right. You'll try.

01:13:13.990 --> 01:13:18.640
I don't want no panic if there's an investigation or even a trial.

01:13:19.610 --> 01:13:21.830
We've been through this kind of thing before. We're still in business.

01:13:22.650 --> 01:13:24.230
A story's printed in a paper.

01:13:24.640 --> 01:13:25.120
So what?

01:13:26.260 --> 01:13:28.360
Tomorrow it's old news. Next week people forget.

01:13:28.580 --> 01:13:29.660
But if they keep printing?

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:30.500
They won't.

01:13:30.580 --> 01:13:33.560
But if they keep us in the news until the trial, if they heat up the public...

01:13:33.560 --> 01:13:35.880
You take care of your end, I'll handle the paper.

01:13:36.100 --> 01:13:37.280
Hutchison won't handle these.

01:13:37.320 --> 01:13:40.130
He's got nothing. With Schmidt out of the way, what's he got?

01:13:40.230 --> 01:13:40.950
That won't stop him.

01:13:41.010 --> 01:13:42.110
And he won't stop us.

01:13:44.290 --> 01:13:45.610
Tomorrow he won't even have a paper.

01:13:46.230 --> 01:13:47.390
Courts will take it away from him.

01:13:48.940 --> 01:13:52.560
And if they don't, we'll take him away from the paper.

01:13:53.740 --> 01:13:55.260
Maybe they all need an example.

01:13:56.660 --> 01:13:57.820
Yeah, that's what they need.

01:14:03.840 --> 01:14:07.220
You'd better find Hutchison. Do you want to see him? No.

01:14:27.720 --> 01:14:28.640
Please rise.

01:14:37.710 --> 01:14:38.730
His honor, the surrogate.

01:14:39.340 --> 01:14:40.060
Please be seated.

01:14:55.220 --> 01:14:58.600
Regarding the sale and purchase of the publishing company,

01:14:59.400 --> 01:14:59.980
you're in reference.

01:15:04.590 --> 01:15:07.830
I've made a careful study of the existing contract

01:15:07.830 --> 01:15:10.540
between the heirs of the late John Garrison

01:15:10.540 --> 01:15:13.160
and Lawrence White Publishing Enterprises.

01:15:14.020 --> 01:15:16.860
I can see no reason why this contract should not be enforced.

01:15:17.930 --> 01:15:21.230
Therefore, unless further evidence or argument

01:15:21.230 --> 01:15:23.170
is presented to alter my judgment,

01:15:24.130 --> 01:15:26.450
the court is prepared to render its decision.

01:15:27.960 --> 01:15:29.970
Mrs. Garrison, do you...

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:31.080
Do you have anything to add?

01:15:33.780 --> 01:15:34.960
No statement, Your Honor.

01:15:36.840 --> 01:15:40.260
Before you decide, may I say something?

01:15:40.340 --> 01:15:43.550
If, Your Honor, please. I don't think this gentleman is one of the heirs.

01:15:43.870 --> 01:15:45.790
He's not here as amicus curiae,

01:15:45.850 --> 01:15:48.660
and I'm positive he's not here in the interests of Mr. White.

01:15:49.550 --> 01:15:51.030
Whom does he presume to represent?

01:15:51.910 --> 01:15:55.720
Well, sir, I'm trying to save a newspaper.

01:15:55.840 --> 01:15:57.100
Which is not yours in the first place.

01:15:57.220 --> 01:15:59.980
Fact is true. The day consists of a big...

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:01.200
It's a big building. I don't own that.

01:16:02.240 --> 01:16:06.940
It also consists of typewriters, teletypes, presses, newsprint, ink and desks.

01:16:07.470 --> 01:16:10.690
I don't own those either. But this newspaper is more than that.

01:16:10.860 --> 01:16:13.100
We're all aware of what a newspaper consists.

01:16:13.400 --> 01:16:14.900
I'm not so sure about that.

01:16:17.220 --> 01:16:20.570
Today is more than a building. It's people.

01:16:21.930 --> 01:16:25.410
Fifteen hundred men and women whose skill, heart, brains and experience...

01:16:25.410 --> 01:16:26.830
make a great newspaper possible.

01:16:27.660 --> 01:16:29.780
We don't own one stick of furniture in this company.

01:16:30.830 --> 01:16:34.150
but we, along with the 290,000 people who read this paper,

01:16:35.060 --> 01:16:37.320
have a vital interest in whether it lives or dies.

01:16:37.540 --> 01:16:39.120
This is highly irregular procedure.

01:16:39.610 --> 01:16:41.310
So is the murder of a newspaper.

01:16:41.490 --> 01:16:42.950
Aren't you carrying this a bit too far?

01:16:43.050 --> 01:16:45.250
The death of a newspaper sometimes has far-reaching effects.

01:16:45.430 --> 01:16:47.010
Meaning your own pocketbook, in this case.

01:16:47.110 --> 01:16:49.910
In this case, meaning some unfinished business called Rianzi.

01:16:50.010 --> 01:16:51.850
If you read the day, you'd know what I mean.

01:16:52.220 --> 01:16:54.040
I don't care to discuss Mr. Rianzi.

01:16:54.060 --> 01:16:55.060
This newspaper does.

01:16:55.320 --> 01:16:56.840
This doesn't concern us here today.

01:16:57.160 --> 01:16:58.900
It concerns the public every day.

01:16:59.240 --> 01:16:59.980
A newspaper is...

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:03.840
as Mr. White will agree, is published first, last and always in the public interest.

01:17:04.180 --> 01:17:06.400
Yours is not the only newspaper in town.

01:17:06.600 --> 01:17:09.420
Right now, it's the only newspaper willing to expose Rienzi.

01:17:09.780 --> 01:17:11.900
Your Honor, an honest, fearless press

01:17:11.900 --> 01:17:16.200
is the public's first protection against gangsterism, local or international.

01:17:17.060 --> 01:17:20.770
Mr. Hutchinson, though a surrogate's court is informal,

01:17:21.220 --> 01:17:23.200
there are certain rules and procedure.

01:17:23.420 --> 01:17:25.060
May we have your decision now, sir?

01:17:26.650 --> 01:17:29.970
As one of your 290,000 readers, Mr. Hutchinson,

01:17:31.080 --> 01:17:34.820
I rule that you may proceed with your statement.

01:17:35.980 --> 01:17:36.520
Thank you, sir.

01:17:37.390 --> 01:17:40.650
But let's try to keep this from becoming a personal matter, please.

01:17:41.740 --> 01:17:43.900
A newspaper is a very personal matter, sir.

01:17:44.440 --> 01:17:46.320
Ask the people who let us in their homes.

01:17:46.790 --> 01:17:49.470
I've read the day for more than 35 years.

01:17:50.510 --> 01:17:52.550
Before that, I sold it in the streets.

01:17:53.810 --> 01:17:58.920
However, here we're only concerned with the legal aspect of the sale and purchase of property.

01:17:59.560 --> 01:17:59.980
What happened?

01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:01.700
after Mr. White takes possession

01:18:01.700 --> 01:18:04.520
is outside of the jurisdiction of this court.

01:18:04.680 --> 01:18:06.140
Well, in whose jurisdiction is it?

01:18:06.240 --> 01:18:07.020
Just a moment.

01:18:07.630 --> 01:18:10.570
Since when is it immoral for someone to legally purchase a newspaper?

01:18:11.320 --> 01:18:14.900
I don't care if Mr. White buys and runs two papers,

01:18:15.020 --> 01:18:16.860
or 20 papers, or 100 papers.

01:18:16.980 --> 01:18:19.380
Some of the best newspapers in this country are part of a chain.

01:18:20.000 --> 01:18:22.400
But I do care when he buys a newspaper to put it out of business.

01:18:26.040 --> 01:18:28.180
Without competition, there could be no freedom of the press.

01:18:28.710 --> 01:18:29.970
And I'm talking about...

01:18:30.820 --> 01:18:35.510
The right of the public to a marketplace of ideas, news, and opinions.

01:18:36.080 --> 01:18:39.560
Not of one man's, or one leader's, or even one government's.

01:18:40.450 --> 01:18:46.390
I guess that's all I have to say.

01:18:49.560 --> 01:18:53.200
The existing contract is valid, made in good faith.

01:18:55.880 --> 01:18:59.440
As of tomorrow, November 14th, the Lawrence White Publications...

01:19:00.000 --> 01:19:02.070
will assume control of the day.

01:19:02.370 --> 01:19:03.210
Court assurance.

01:19:09.330 --> 01:19:10.560
Thanks for trying.

01:19:11.320 --> 01:19:12.340
It'll be another day.

01:19:13.160 --> 01:19:13.780
Goodbye, Ed.

01:19:20.330 --> 01:19:21.130
Here I am, Mr. Hutchison.

01:19:21.450 --> 01:19:22.070
I have the city desk.

01:19:23.590 --> 01:19:25.490
Frank, here it is.

01:19:26.210 --> 01:19:27.410
Lead-off for the morning edition.

01:19:28.490 --> 01:19:29.970
The day after 47 years...

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:33.020
The news of Daily Publication was sold last night.

01:19:33.300 --> 01:19:34.780
Ed, get back here as quick as you can.

01:19:35.720 --> 01:19:36.840
Yeah, well, give it to me over the phone.

01:19:42.040 --> 01:19:43.120
Be right there. Five minutes.

01:19:46.240 --> 01:19:48.320
Yeah, the state who killed your daughter, Mrs. Schmidt?

01:19:49.010 --> 01:19:49.850
Was it Rienzi?

01:19:50.010 --> 01:19:50.770
Some of his men.

01:19:52.190 --> 01:19:53.750
I come to see boss.

01:19:54.500 --> 01:19:56.380
Did you know your son was working for Rienzi?

01:19:57.580 --> 01:19:58.300
Was that Hatch?

01:20:00.890 --> 01:20:04.920
Do you think we ought to call the police? I'm worried about her.

01:20:05.080 --> 01:20:10.630
It would have been sold.

01:20:41.360 --> 01:20:46.030
Coffee. Have you been to your home yet, Mrs. Schmidt?

01:20:47.920 --> 01:20:51.120
Where have you been? I speak to boss.

01:20:57.790 --> 01:20:59.810
Mrs. Schmidt wandered in on her own.

01:21:00.380 --> 01:21:02.280
Looking for you won't talk to anyone else.

01:21:03.800 --> 01:21:05.740
Mrs. Smith, the boss.

01:21:06.300 --> 01:21:07.400
Oh, how do you do, Mrs. Smith?

01:21:08.150 --> 01:21:09.010
Your name, please?

01:21:09.490 --> 01:21:09.890
Hutcherson.

01:21:11.440 --> 01:21:12.720
I am mother to Besson.

01:21:16.590 --> 01:21:19.150
What's your son? I'm very sorry.

01:21:19.800 --> 01:21:21.020
I do not comfort that.

01:21:29.560 --> 01:21:29.900
Sit down.

01:21:30.030 --> 01:21:39.140
Here, my Bessie. She comes to me and she says,

01:21:40.280 --> 01:21:42.640
Here, Mama, you keep this.

01:21:43.750 --> 01:21:47.510
If something happens to me, you do not have to worry.

01:21:50.010 --> 01:21:50.790
Bessie's diary?

01:21:51.470 --> 01:21:56.650
She says what happens to her and this Mr. Rienzi.

01:22:02.040 --> 01:22:07.670
Get Captain Finley over here right away and tell Arlen we're getting out the final edition, as usual.

01:22:08.250 --> 01:22:08.910
Yes, sir.

01:22:12.440 --> 01:22:14.060
Why didn't you go to the police, Mrs. Schmidt?

01:22:15.000 --> 01:22:17.980
Police? I do not know police.

01:22:19.270 --> 01:22:22.100
I know newspaper, this newspaper.

01:22:23.330 --> 01:22:26.200
For 31 years, I know this paper.

01:22:27.210 --> 01:22:29.980
I come to America. I wish to be...

01:22:30.160 --> 01:22:33.470
How to do this? From newspaper.

01:22:34.680 --> 01:22:36.400
It shows me how to read and write.

01:22:38.180 --> 01:22:42.050
My Bessie dies. You do not say bad things of her.

01:22:43.160 --> 01:22:45.060
You do not show bad pictures of her.

01:22:46.620 --> 01:22:50.290
You try to find who hurt my Bessie.

01:22:54.020 --> 01:22:56.530
I help. I think what to do.

01:22:57.390 --> 01:22:59.970
I go on subway. I write all day.

01:23:05.500 --> 01:23:08.460
By doing this, you may be in danger, like your son.

01:23:09.720 --> 01:23:13.800
You're not afraid. Your paper's not afraid.

01:23:30.280 --> 01:23:33.600
Oh, Mrs. Hutchison. Or is it Mrs. Schaefer now?

01:23:33.860 --> 01:23:35.260
Where is he? In the press room.

01:23:35.440 --> 01:23:40.460
Has he lost the paper? Yes.

01:23:41.160 --> 01:23:43.990
What's he gonna do? Get out the last edition.

01:23:44.740 --> 01:23:46.310
And it ought to be quite a paper.

01:23:46.970 --> 01:23:49.530
But then what? Look for another job, I guess.

01:23:50.510 --> 01:23:51.790
He's it, Mrs. Schaefer.

01:23:52.090 --> 01:23:59.020
Hello, Alex.

01:24:00.000 --> 01:24:02.240
This is it, huh? Yeah. Looks like the last one.

01:24:05.180 --> 01:24:27.430
Good morning, Mr. Hutchison. Yes?

01:24:32.080 --> 01:24:33.020
Put the call through.

01:24:40.300 --> 01:24:40.680
Hutchison?

01:24:41.270 --> 01:24:42.230
Hello, baby.

01:24:44.740 --> 01:24:45.440
How am I feeling?

01:24:47.130 --> 01:24:48.810
I hear Mrs. Schmidt come in to see you.

01:24:49.350 --> 01:24:49.810
That's right.

01:24:50.870 --> 01:24:51.530
That's right.

01:24:53.040 --> 01:24:55.080
There's some loose cash here belongs to you.

01:24:55.240 --> 01:24:56.780
$200,000 worth.

01:24:58.270 --> 01:24:59.850
And there's something else, too.

01:25:05.050 --> 01:25:05.770
What diary?

01:25:08.060 --> 01:25:10.540
Who's gonna believe what a little tramp writes to herself?

01:25:11.500 --> 01:25:12.440
Wait a minute, don't hang up.

01:25:13.360 --> 01:25:14.700
Here's some advice for your friend.

01:25:15.690 --> 01:25:16.630
Don't press your luck.

01:25:17.230 --> 01:25:18.030
Lay off of me.

01:25:18.880 --> 01:25:20.080
Don't print that story.

01:25:20.420 --> 01:25:21.860
What's that supposed to be?

01:25:22.200 --> 01:25:22.780
An order?

01:25:23.220 --> 01:25:24.800
If not tonight, then tomorrow.

01:25:25.190 --> 01:25:26.710
Maybe next week, maybe next year.

01:25:27.640 --> 01:25:29.040
But sooner or later, you'll catch it.

01:25:29.500 --> 01:25:29.980
Listen to me.

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:32.910
Print that story or a dead man.

01:25:33.630 --> 01:25:34.730
It's not just me anymore.

01:25:36.230 --> 01:25:38.230
You'd have to stop every newspaper in the country now,

01:25:38.370 --> 01:25:39.550
and you're not big enough at that job.

01:25:39.900 --> 01:25:41.200
People like you've tried it before,

01:25:41.500 --> 01:25:42.810
with bullets, prison censorship.

01:25:43.770 --> 01:25:46.630
As long as even one newspaper will print the truth, you're finished.

01:25:47.150 --> 01:25:48.990
Don't give me that fancy double talk.

01:25:49.130 --> 01:25:50.750
I want an answer. Yes or no?

01:26:05.520 --> 01:26:15.350
Hey, Hutchison, that noise, what's that racket?

01:26:15.830 --> 01:26:18.340
That's the press, baby, the press.

01:26:19.230 --> 01:26:21.220
There's nothing you can do about it, nothing.
